__d(function(e,a,o,t,n,s,i){n.exports={checked:"28 October 2026",summaries:{r7311:{overview:"This bill makes the related legal changes for a new national code on gender-based violence at universities, including making compliance with the code a condition for approved higher education providers. It was debated together with the main bill, which lets the Education Minister set the code and creates a new enforcement unit in the Department of Education. All parties supported the bills, while the debate also covered who should enforce the code and whether a similar code was needed for antisemitism.",for:["Members across parties cited surveys showing one in 20 students reported sexual assault and many felt unheard when they complained.","Government members said the code makes vice-chancellors accountable, bans most non-disclosure agreements and covers student housing run by private colleges.","Supporters said universities and the existing regulator had failed to act, so enforceable national standards with penalties were needed.","The Greens, independents and Coalition speakers praised advocates and survivors whose campaigning led to the reform."],against:["Coalition speakers said putting the regulator inside the Education Department, not the independent regulator TEQSA, risks politicising enforcement.","Coalition senators and Kate Chaney questioned leaving the code's detail to the minister, as a Senate committee had noted.","Kate Chaney and Zali Steggall said universities and smaller student housing providers need more funding, guidance and clear rules to comply.","The Coalition moved an amendment calling for a similar code on antisemitism, which Allegra Spender supported. The Government and Greens did not support it."],speeches:46,speakers:35,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28816/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_23_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28819/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28820/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28822/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_31_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-08-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28830/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7312:{overview:"This bill sets up a national code that all universities and higher education providers must follow to prevent and respond to gender-based violence. It includes rules for student housing, a ban on most non-disclosure agreements, and a new enforcement branch in the Department of Education. The transcript includes only the Education Minister's introduction speech.",for:["The Education Minister said surveys show one in 20 students reported being sexually assaulted on campus and many felt unheard when they complained.","He said the code would make all providers meet the same high standards, with vice-chancellors directly accountable and reporting every six months.","The Government said universities could no longer leave disclosures to private colleges and would have to investigate reports involving their students or staff.","The minister said the code was shaped by wide consultation with victim-survivors, students, experts, the sector and state governments.","The Government said strong powers, civil penalties and yearly public reporting would build a culture of compliance."],against:[],speeches:1,speakers:1,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-08-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28830/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7335:{overview:"This bill adds a rule to the Fair Work Act saying that when the Fair Work Commission makes or changes modern awards, it must not cut penalty or overtime rates, or allow them to be rolled into a single rate that leaves any employee worse off. The Government said it was needed because employer groups had applications before the Commission covering the retail, banking and clerical awards. The debate focused on whether the bill protects low-paid workers or limits choice and flexibility, the Commission's independence, possible effects on existing awards, and small business.",for:["The Government said about 2.6 million award workers, many of them women, young people and casuals, rely on penalty rates to pay bills and should not go backwards.","Government members said current applications could leave some retail workers more than $5,000 a year worse off, so the law needed to change quickly.","The Government said the bill adds no new obligations on businesses, does not apply backwards, and leaves enterprise bargaining and individual flexibility arrangements unchanged.","The Greens, One Nation and Senator Tyrrell supported the bill, though the Greens called it a defensive step and Senator Tyrrell wanted a regulatory impact statement."],against:["Coalition speakers said they support penalty rates but the bill is unnecessary because the Commission already protects them, and it undermines the Commission's independence.","Coalition speakers said the bill removes workers' choice to take a higher, steadier base salary, which can help with loans and Centrelink reporting.","The Coalition said there was no regulatory impact statement or small business consultation, and moved amendments for a small business exemption and an impact statement.","Independents Kate Chaney and Allegra Spender opposed the bill, saying it pre-empts the Commission and adds complexity; Ms Spender urged making productivity an objective of awards.","The Greens moved amendments for a right to work from home and wider rights to request flexible work; the Government opposed them as outside the bill's scope."],speeches:442,speakers:53,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28811/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28815/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_31_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-08-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28831/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_08_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-08-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28834/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_08_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-08-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28837/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_08_27_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-08-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28841/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_08_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7336:{overview:"This bill lets the federal government suspend or cut off the childcare subsidy to providers that do not meet safety and quality standards, and stop them opening new centres. It also lets federal officers make surprise visits without a warrant and lets the department publish more about action taken against providers. The debate followed alleged abuse in Victorian childcare centres, and all parties who spoke supported the bill while saying more work was needed.",for:["Government members said the $16 billion childcare subsidy is the Commonwealth's biggest lever, and tying it to safety will push providers to lift their standards.","Coalition speakers supported the bill, saying unannounced visits stop providers cleaning up before inspections and parents deserve to know about breaches.","Supporters said publishing conditions and fines helps parents choose a centre and holds company boards to account.","The government said providers get 28 days to respond and a chance to improve, so the aim is raising standards, not closing centres."],against:["Coalition speakers said the bill was prepared quickly and does not define 'persistent' noncompliance, which could make decisions unclear for providers.","Coalition senators said most safety powers sit with the states, and urged faster national work on working-with-children checks, a worker register and phone bans.","The Greens supported the bill but called it a small step, and moved an amendment for an independent national commission and a free, universal system.","Liberal Senator Leah Blyth warned that more red tape could raise costs and drive good workers out, and urged more support for family choice in care."],speeches:37,speakers:16,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28810/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_23_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28813/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28821/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28822/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_31_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7337:{overview:"This bill lets Defence Housing Australia provide housing to more groups, including foreign military personnel, foreign officials and contractors, and charities that support Defence. The Government said its first big use would be housing US and UK submarine crews and families near HMAS Stirling in Western Australia under AUKUS. The debate focused on AUKUS, the cost of the housing, and pressure on local housing markets.",for:["The Government said the change follows an Auditor-General recommendation and reflects a Defence workforce that now relies on foreign partners and outside organisations.","Government senators said using Defence Housing would meet Defence standards while limiting pressure on local housing markets, with new homes already contracted near Perth.","The Coalition supported the bill, saying housing allied submarine crews is the first practical test of AUKUS on Australian soil.","Labor speakers said AUKUS would bring jobs, skills and investment, especially in Perth's southern suburbs.","One Nation said it would support the bill because Australia relies on allies for its defence."],against:["The Greens opposed the bill, saying public money should build homes for Australians in a housing crisis, not for foreign troops and weapons contractors.","The Greens said the bill had no cost estimate or public inquiry, and that funding would depend on undisclosed AUKUS cost-sharing deals.","The Greens also opposed AUKUS itself, saying it ties Australia to US military plans, and moved amendments to remove the new foreign categories.","Coalition senators backed the bill but said it brought no new funding or housing supply and could crowd out housing for Australian personnel.","Senator Lambie moved an amendment for Australian personnel to get priority, for foreign governments to cover full costs, and for more public data."],speeches:104,speakers:29,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28811/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28822/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_31_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-08-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28831/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_08_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7338:{overview:"This bill extends ASIO's compulsory questioning powers by 18 months, until 7 March 2027, so they do not lapse while a second bill with bigger reforms is considered. The debate was short, with Government and Coalition members both supporting it, and the parliamentary intelligence committee recommending it pass.",for:["The Government said the extension keeps ASIO's powers in place while reforms are developed and gives the intelligence committee time to review them.","The Government said the powers stay subject to safeguards, including oversight by the Inspector-General of Intelligence and Security.","Coalition speakers said the powers have had bipartisan support since 2003 and remain a useful tool against terrorism, espionage and foreign interference.","Nationals members said Australia faces growing threats from foreign powers and new technology like artificial intelligence, so ASIO needs strong powers.","The intelligence committee said the extension lets it finish an in-depth review it began in the previous parliament."],against:[],speeches:8,speakers:7,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28810/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_23_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28815/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_31_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-08-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28839/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28853/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7339:{overview:"This bill changes ASIO's power to compel people to attend and answer questions. As introduced, it made the power permanent by removing its sunset date, added new grounds such as sabotage, promotion of communal violence and threats to border integrity, and tightened some safeguards. The debate focused on whether these extraordinary powers should be permanent and wider, and on their use for children aged 14 to 17; in the Senate the Government agreed to keep a three-year sunset and a committee review after two years.",for:["The Government said the security environment is worsening, and ASIO needs these powers to respond to a wider range of modern threats.","Government and Coalition speakers said the powers have been used only about 20 times in 22 years, showing restraint rather than overreach.","Supporters pointed to safeguards: Attorney-General approval, an independent prescribed authority, oversight by the Inspector-General, and questioning of charged people only before a retired judge.","The Government said a growing share of counter-terrorism cases involve minors, so it kept the power to question 14 to 17 year olds.","Coalition senators supported the bill once the Government agreed to restore a three-year sunset clause."],against:["Independents Kate Chaney, Allegra Spender and Zali Steggall moved amendments to keep a sunset or require regular review, saying extraordinary powers need regular checks.","Critics said ASIO and Home Affairs had earlier said border security grounds and questioning of minors were not needed.","The Greens, Senator Pocock and Senator Antic opposed the bill, citing people questioned without suspicion, limits on lawyers, and no judge approving warrants.","One Nation said 'promotion of communal violence' was too vague, moved amendments including one to name radical Islamic extremism, and opposed the bill.","Greens senators objected to the Senate debate being limited by a guillotine."],speeches:96,speakers:38,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28810/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_23_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29201/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29138/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29139/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29205/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-05-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29220/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_05_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29235/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7342:{overview:"This bill cuts all existing student loan debts, including HELP, VET and apprenticeship loans, by 20 per cent based on balances at 1 June 2025, wiping about $16 billion. It also lifts the income at which repayments start from about $54,000 to $67,000 and changes repayments so they are only charged on income above that level. The Coalition did not oppose it, and much of the debate was about who benefits, the cost to taxpayers and wider reforms such as indexation timing and Job-ready Graduates fees.",for:["The government said it delivers an election promise and cuts the average debt of about $27,600 by around $5,500 for three million people.","Government members said the new repayment system is fairer and gives someone earning $70,000 about $1,300 a year more in take-home pay.","Supporters said student debt weighs on young people trying to rent, buy a home or start a family, and the cut also covers TAFE and apprentices.","Crossbench MPs and senators supported the bill as welcome relief while calling for further reforms."],against:["Coalition speakers said it is poorly targeted, gives the most to people with big debts, and is paid for by taxpayers who never went to university.","Coalition and One Nation speakers said it does nothing for past or future students, and low earners may take longer to repay.","Crossbench members and the Greens said Job-ready Graduates fees should be reversed and indexation should apply after repayments are counted.","The Greens moved to wipe all student debt and end indexation, and Liberal Senator Sarah Henderson moved to cap indexation at 3 per cent; the government opposed these.","One Nation opposed the bill, calling it a vote-buying measure."],speeches:122,speakers:57,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28810/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_23_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28812/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28813/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28820/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28821/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28822/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_31_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7343:{overview:"This bill makes technical and transitional changes so the new Aged Care Act 2024 can start on 1 November 2025. It covers things like correct subsidy payments, protecting people already in care from paying more, moving unspent home care funds across, removing caps on cleaning and gardening, and a temporary power for the minister to fix problems by rules. The debate focused mostly on the long waitlist for home care packages, and the Senate added requests to release 20,000 packages straight away and 83,000 places in total that year.",for:["Government members said the bill is needed to start a new rights-based aged care system recommended by the royal commission.","Supporters said it protects people already in care through a 'no worse off' principle and carries unspent home care funds into the new system.","Coalition, Greens and crossbench speakers welcomed removing caps on cleaning and gardening services.","Government senators said a temporary rule-making power helps keep care going if unexpected problems arise during the changeover, with parliament still able to disallow rules."],against:["Coalition speakers said the bill fixes 325 items in Labor's own law and shows the government was not ready for its original 1 July start date.","The Coalition, Greens and Senator David Pocock said home care packages were being withheld while over 87,000 assessed people waited, and moved amendments to release them sooner.","Independents and the Greens raised concerns about the minister's power to change the Act by rules, and about automated decisions.","The Greens said the bill favours providers, including fees for missed visits, and moved unsuccessful amendments on hospital cancellations, provider offences and whistleblowers.","The Greens and Senator Lidia Thorpe sought to exclude redress payments from means tests; the government and Coalition said this should be done across all government payments instead."],speeches:151,speakers:38,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28811/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28822/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_31_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-08-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28834/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_08_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28844/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_01_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28847/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_02_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28850/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28852/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28853/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7344:{overview:"This bill makes technical and consequential changes to the aged care accommodation payment levy law to fit the new Aged Care Act 2024. The minister said it keeps registered providers under the government's guarantee of payments made by older Australians, and lets the government levy the cost of that guarantee from some providers, such as after a default. The transcript records only the introduction and the bill passing both houses, with no debate.",for:["The minister said the bill keeps aged care services sustainable and keeps payments made by older Australians protected into the future."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28811/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28850/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7345:{overview:"This bill cuts the most a general patient pays for a Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme medicine from $31.60 to $25 per script from 1 January 2026, the lowest since 2004. Pharmacies can still discount below that price. Every party supported the bill, and the debate focused on cost-of-living pressures, delays in listing new medicines, and medicine shortages.",for:["Government members said people are skipping or rationing medicines because of cost, and the cut will save Australians over $200 million a year.","Labor speakers said cheaper medicines keep people healthier and out of hospital, which is good for the health system and the economy.","The Coalition supported the bill, saying it matched its own election promise of a $25 co-payment.","Supporters said four in five PBS medicines will become cheaper, building on earlier cuts and 60-day prescriptions."],against:["The Coalition moved a second reading amendment criticising delays in acting on the health technology assessment review and in listing new medicines.","Independents Kate Chaney and Monique Ryan supported the bill but said the safety net remains high and approval of new medicines is too slow.","Senator Fatima Payman said hundreds of medicines are in short supply and moved to cut the co-payment to $20, which the Greens supported. The government opposed it.","The Greens said the bill does not go far enough and called for dental care to be covered by Medicare.","One Nation senators questioned rising scheme costs, possible Medicare card fraud and the use of cheaper generic brands."],speeches:159,speakers:69,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-08-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28833/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-08-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28836/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_27_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-08-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28839/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-08-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28841/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_08_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28844/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_01_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7351:{overview:"This bill updates the 1998 law on payment systems so the Reserve Bank can regulate newer services like digital wallets and buy now, pay later. It also lets the Treasurer flag payment services that pose national-level risks and adds new civil penalties. Debate focused on whether parliament should be able to review and overturn decisions made by the Reserve Bank and the Treasurer under the law.",for:["Government members said the old law was written before smartphones and digital wallets, so it leaves gaps that create risks for consumers and businesses.","Labor speakers said new definitions are technology-neutral, so new payment methods are covered from the start rather than after problems arise.","Government members said a national interest power lets the Treasurer respond to risks like cyber threats, data misuse and national security.","Labor speakers said safeguards apply: the Treasurer must consult regulators and can only give system-wide directions, keeping regulators independent.","Coalition speakers said they supported the bill's aims and would not oppose it if their amendments failed."],against:["Coalition speakers said the Reserve Bank's and Treasurer's key decisions should be open to parliamentary disallowance, since the law now covers many more businesses.","Coalition members argued the Reserve Bank's narrow focus may not suit fast-changing fintech and could unintentionally favour big banks over start-ups.","Coalition member Tim Wilson said the bill overreaches into areas like blockchain and digital wallets and relies too much on ministerial regulation.","Some Coalition members said the House should also focus on card surcharges, merchant fees and the collapse of certain super funds.","The Senate agreed to opposition amendments making some decisions legislative instruments; the Greens moved amendments to limit Reserve Bank board conflicts of interest."],speeches:26,speakers:16,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28846/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_02_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28852/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28853/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7352:{overview:"This bill approves $195.1 million to run the parliament's own departments for the rest of 2025-26, mostly for the Department of Parliamentary Services. It was debated together with the main budget spending bills, so almost all speeches were about the federal budget in general rather than this bill. The Coalition said it would not oppose the bills but criticised the government's economic management.",for:["Government members said the budget bills fund cost-of-living help, including tax cuts, energy bill relief, cheaper medicines and a 20 per cent cut to student debt.","Labor speakers pointed to more Medicare urgent care clinics, bulk-billing and housing support as reasons to back the bills.","Government members said they had delivered budget surpluses, lower inflation and low unemployment.","Independent Helen Haines welcomed funding for the Cheaper Home Batteries Program in the bills."],against:["The Coalition did not oppose the bills but said government spending and debt are too high and living standards have fallen.","Coalition speakers called for fiscal rules to limit spending, and criticised energy costs and the renewables rollout.","Coalition members said regional roads, water and infrastructure were not getting enough funding.","Helen Haines said some election commitments were funded as budget line items without an open grants process, which she called poor practice."],speeches:34,speakers:26,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28843/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_01_Official.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28846/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_02_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28849/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28852/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28901/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_09_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28859/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7353:{overview:"This bill is one of the main bills for the government's 2025-26 budget. It seeks approval to spend $14.6 billion on services that are not ordinary annual services, including about $7.2 billion for Defence and $2.4 billion for infrastructure and communications projects. The transcript covers its introduction and passage, with no debate.",for:["The government said the funding supports defence capability, Inland Rail, NBN upgrades, Western Sydney airport and local roads programs."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:3,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28901/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_09_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7354:{overview:"This is the main budget spending bill for 2025-26, approving about $83.4 billion for the day-to-day running of government, including Defence, health, the NDIS, foreign affairs and Home Affairs. The debate was a portfolio-by-portfolio 'consideration in detail', where members questioned and defended spending in education, defence, the economy, workplace relations, agriculture, climate and energy, justice, communications, industry, home affairs, social services, health and Indigenous affairs. Coalition members also complained that several senior ministers did not attend to answer questions.",for:["Government members said inflation and interest rates are down, over a million jobs were created, and two budget surpluses were delivered while giving cost-of-living help.","Government speakers highlighted extra school funding, cheaper child care, a 20 per cent cut to student debts, bulk-billing incentives, urgent care clinics and cheaper medicines.","The Government said it is lifting Defence spending, including $12 billion for the Henderson shipyard, and supporting recruitment and veterans' claims.","Government members pointed to renewable energy approvals, home battery discounts, critical minerals support and more biosecurity funding for farmers.","Government speakers also cited longer paid parental leave with super, over $4 billion to address gender-based violence and record legal assistance funding."],against:["The Coalition said the budget had worsened by $58 billion over two years, debt was nearing $1 trillion and spending was growing faster than the economy.","Coalition speakers said living costs, energy prices and GP out-of-pocket costs had risen, and that apprentice numbers had fallen by about 100,000.","The Coalition said Defence was underfunded, criticised the live sheep export ban and tribunal backlogs, and raised questions about the return of 'ISIS brides'.","Independents called for fiscal rules, revenue-neutral tax reform, more disaster and climate resilience funding, and more spending on preventive health and integrity agencies.","The Greens raised concerns about weapons parts exports to Israel and possible US government stakes in Australian critical minerals."],speeches:152,speakers:93,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28814/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-07",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28899/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_07_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28901/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_09_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7358:{overview:"This bill gives the telecommunications regulator, ACMA, stronger powers over phone and internet companies. It raises the maximum fine for breaking industry rules from $250,000 to about $10 million or more, makes industry codes directly enforceable, and creates a register of all service providers. The Coalition supported the bill, and much of the debate turned to poor mobile coverage in regional areas.",for:["The Government said current fines are so small that big telcos treat them as a cost of doing business.","Supporters said removing the warning-first step lets ACMA act straight away on breaches, such as scam call and emergency database rules.","Members said a provider register would show who is operating and let ACMA remove providers that seriously harm customers, as happens in energy.","Coalition speakers supported the bill, noting industry and consumer groups had asked for these changes.","Labor members cited cases of telcos selling unsuitable plans to vulnerable people as reasons for tougher rules."],against:["Nobody opposed the bill. The Coalition moved an amendment criticising the 3G network shutdown and the Government's late response to a regional telecommunications review. The Government opposed it.","Coalition and independent members said the bill does nothing to fix poor mobile and internet coverage in regional areas, and called for an updated universal service obligation.","Coalition member Tim Wilson said he supported the bill but urged caution that telecommunications powers should not be used to limit free speech."],speeches:44,speakers:31,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-08-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28839/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28852/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29136/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_09.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29153/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29155/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_13.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29156/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-22",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29221/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_22.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7359:{overview:"This bill changes how the Defence Honours and Awards Appeals Tribunal reviews decisions to refuse military medals. It would limit most reviews to service within 20 years of an operation ending, narrow who can apply, and set a six-month deadline to seek a review. The debate focused on whether these limits were needed and whether they would stop past heroes being recognised; a Coalition member later said the bill was discharged from the Senate.",for:["The government said the tribunal finds it hard to judge actions from as far back as 1939, when records are poor and witnesses have died.","The minister said limiting applicants stops historians with no link to a veteran from seeking reviews that can upset families.","Government members said a six-month deadline, with exceptions for things like serious illness, is longer than for most government reviews.","The minister said the changes only apply to the future, keep the tribunal independent, and still let ministers refer any matter for inquiry."],against:["Coalition members said the tribunal works well and the government had not shown what problem the bill fixes.","Coalition speakers said cases like Teddy Sheean's Victoria Cross and the Long Tan medals could not have been reviewed under a 20-year limit.","Opponents said the tribunal itself opposed the 20-year limit, and veterans groups such as the RSL said they were not properly consulted.","The Coalition moved amendments to remove the 20-year limit, keep broad rights to apply, and require people to be told of their review rights.","Independents Allegra Spender and Helen Haines said they could not support the bill as it stood and backed the Coalition amendments."],speeches:42,speakers:15,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-08-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28839/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28849/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28853/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28855/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_27_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7360:{overview:"This bill changes surveillance, interception and crime laws so agencies can use their powers as Parliament intended. It lets warrant information be disclosed in court, moves a regulatory role to Home Affairs, allows stored messages to be used for testing, adjusts international data orders with the US, and clarifies protections for undercover officers. Labor and the Coalition supported it, while the Greens opposed it and tried to delay it and remove the international orders part.",for:["The Government said the changes are technical fixes that let agencies keep up with fast-changing technology while keeping strict safeguards.","Government members said allowing network warrant information to be disclosed helps prosecutions and supports a fair trial for accused people.","Coalition and Government speakers said clearer legal protection for undercover officers will help take down online child sexual abuse networks, with reports at about 226 a day.","The Government said the US data order change only fixes how data is delivered and does not let the US get more information about Australians.","Coalition speakers supported the bill but said the Home Affairs change was only needed because Labor had split up that department in 2022."],against:["The Greens said the bill greatly expands surveillance powers without proper safeguards, and that calling it technical was misleading.","The Greens and the Internet Association of Australia said it should wait for a recent watchdog report and a Home Affairs review of surveillance laws.","The Greens said letting agencies test with stored communications means real people's private messages could be accessed with little oversight.","Citing a human rights committee report, the Greens said the US orders change could greatly broaden data shared, including about people not under investigation.","The Greens moved to delay the bill until full consultation occurred, and to remove the US data schedule; both moves were not adopted."],speeches:42,speakers:13,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-08-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28836/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_27_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-07",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28899/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_07_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28863/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7363:{overview:"This bill says procedural fairness does not apply to certain steps in removing non-citizens who have no right to stay, such as arranging their transfer to a third country like Nauru or sharing their information with foreign governments. It also makes past visa decisions made before the High Court's NZYQ ruling valid, even though they relied on an old understanding of the law. The debate focused on community safety, the right to be heard, how many people the bill could cover, the cost of the Nauru deal, and how quickly the bill was passed.",for:["The Government said people near the end of the removal process were using procedural fairness to delay their removal, and the bill puts a Federal Court ruling beyond doubt.","The Government said the bill does not affect fairness in visa refusal or cancellation decisions, merits review, or protections against returning people to harm.","Coalition speakers supported the bill, saying it stops members of the NZYQ cohort with serious criminal records using the courts to delay removal.","One Nation's Malcolm Roberts supported the bill as giving a real chance of removing released people who had committed crimes."],against:["Independents and the Greens said removing the right to be heard is a serious step, as people could not raise family, health or safety risks before removal.","Critics said the bill could reach far more than the NZYQ cohort, with some estimates of up to 80,000 people on bridging visas.","Several crossbench MPs said making past decisions valid after the fact undermines the rule of law, and that the bill was rushed without a committee inquiry.","Coalition senators backed the bill but criticised a lack of detail on the Nauru deal, and moved amendments to lower the bar for preventive detention and tighten mandatory sentencing.","The Greens and some independents raised concerns about Nauru, Australia's international obligations, and the cost of the arrangement, and the Greens moved amendments to remove parts of the bill."],speeches:76,speakers:30,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-08-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28833/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-08-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28841/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_08_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28843/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_01_Official.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28846/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_02_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28847/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_02_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28850/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28853/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7365:{overview:"This bill lets the Administrative Review Tribunal decide more cases using written material only, without an oral hearing. It requires reviews of refused student visas, and other temporary visas set by regulation, to be decided on the papers, but not permanent or protection visas. The debate focused on the tribunal's large backlog and whether removing hearings is fair to applicants.",for:["Government members said a surge in student visa appeals, now about 40 per cent of cases, was causing long delays for everyone.","Supporters said long waits encourage non-genuine applicants to appeal just to stay longer on bridging visas.","The government said applicants can still give unlimited written evidence and must be shown adverse information to respond to.","Coalition speakers supported the bill, saying faster written reviews worked under their earlier fast-track system and help clear backlogs.","Some Labor members said written reviews may be less stressful for people nervous about appearing in person."],against:["The Greens opposed the bill, saying it removes fair hearing rights and that the real causes are too few tribunal members and poor visa refusal decisions.","Critics noted almost half of student visa refusals are overturned on review, so the cases are not always simple.","Independent members and senators said young applicants and people with limited English may struggle to make their case in writing.","Crossbench members moved amendments to let the tribunal hold hearings when needed, require a review of the changes, and stop expansion by regulation.","Some speakers warned the change could lead to more court appeals and shift work onto under-funded community legal services."],speeches:88,speakers:34,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-09-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28849/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28855/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_27_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28869/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28870/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29199/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29200/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29201/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7366:{overview:"This bill makes several technical health changes. It lets computers issue Medicare provider numbers in simple cases, fixes how the private health insurance rebate is processed, delays new bulk-billing paperwork rules to July 2026, and changes penalties in the Bonded Medical Program for doctors who agree to work in the regions. Most debate was about the Bonded Medical Program changes and the state of bulk-billing.",for:["Government members said faster provider numbers would let doctors, including overseas-trained doctors who can wait up to three months, start treating patients sooner.","The Government said removing the six-year Medicare ban for bonded doctors would keep doctors working in communities that need them.","The Government said students who leave medicine before finishing should not face debts of $78,000 or more with no degree.","Supporters said the rebate changes make the $7 billion a year in payments lawful and recoverable, and the delay gives clinics time to update software.","The Greens supported aligning the rebate system and used the debate to call for Medicare to cover prosthetics after head and neck cancer."],against:["The Coalition did not oppose the bill in the House but wanted a Senate inquiry, saying the bonded program changes lacked detail.","Coalition members said the changes could weaken a program that gets doctors into regional areas and let people take benefits without meeting their obligation.","The Coalition moved Senate amendments to remove the bonded program changes, which the Government opposed.","Coalition speakers said bulk-billing had fallen and out-of-pocket costs had risen under the Government.","One Nation senator Malcolm Roberts asked about the use of computers or AI in decisions, and the minister said AI was not required."],speeches:43,speakers:19,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28852/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-07",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28899/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_07_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28867/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7367:{overview:"This bill bundles seven changes, including tighter rules on disclosing who owns shares in listed companies, letting the charities regulator speak publicly about investigations, and reviewing ASIC and APRA every five years instead of every two. It also extends energy market misconduct rules to 2031 and the $20,000 instant asset write-off for small business to June 2026. Debate centred on the review cycle for regulators and on making the write-off permanent.",for:["Government members said clearer ownership disclosure will show who really controls listed companies and help tackle tax avoidance and financial crime.","Labor speakers said letting the charities regulator confirm investigations will protect public trust and encourage more giving.","The government said five-year reviews of ASIC and APRA would be more thorough and give regulators time to act on recommendations.","Government members said an independent review found the energy misconduct rules worked, so extending them protects households and businesses.","Speakers from all sides, including the Greens and independents, supported extending the instant asset write-off to help small business cash flow."],against:["Coalition speakers said fewer reviews of ASIC and APRA would reduce accountability, especially after investors lost over $1 billion in the First Guardian and Shield collapses.","Independents Sophie Scamps and Allegra Spender questioned the five-year review cycle, noting a Senate committee had recommended keeping two-year reviews.","Independents said extending the write-off one year at a time creates uncertainty and moved amendments to make it permanent and raise it to $50,000.","The Greens said the ownership changes were only a small step and called for a full public register covering private companies and trusts.","The Coalition and Greens supported the bill in the House but reserved their final position pending a Senate committee inquiry."],speeches:32,speakers:22,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28852/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-07",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28899/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_07_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7368:{overview:"This bill changes who must pay the levy that funds the National Joint Replacement Registry, which tracks how hip, knee and other joint implants perform. It makes the company currently responsible for a listed device pay, not just the original applicant, and confirms past levy collections were valid. All parties who spoke supported it, and much of the debate turned to wider issues such as hospital funding and aged care.",for:["Government members said the registry tracks which implants work and which fail, helping surgeons and regulators keep patients safe.","Speakers said the change keeps up with companies merging or selling products, so the levy falls on whoever is now responsible for the device.","Labor members said companies that profit from devices, not patients or taxpayers, should fund the system that monitors them.","Supporters said validating past payments protects the registry's funding from legal challenge.","The Coalition and the Greens said it was a sensible technical fix and supported it."],against:["Nobody opposed the bill itself. Coalition speakers used the debate to criticise the government on hospital funding, surgery waiting lists and home-care packages.","In the Senate the Coalition moved a second reading amendment criticising the government over hospital funding."],speeches:14,speakers:10,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28852/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28863/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28867/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7369:{overview:"This bill sets up the Australian Centre for Disease Control from 1 January 2026, an independent agency led by a director-general to advise governments, share health data and prepare for future pandemics, as recommended by the COVID-19 inquiry. The debate covered whether the centre is needed, its transparency and data-sharing powers, and whether it should cover chronic disease and climate health from the start. Labor and the Greens supported it, the Coalition and One Nation opposed it, and the Senate added Greens amendments including a seat for a disabled person on its advisory council.",for:["The Government said the COVID-19 inquiry found Australia had no pandemic playbook, weak data sharing and no national public health body.","Government members said Australia was the only OECD country without such an agency, and its advice would be independent and published.","Labor speakers said better linked data across states would help detect risks faster and give consistent advice across borders.","Government members said starting with infectious diseases and reviewing in 2028 would set the centre up to succeed before widening its role.","Greens and independent members supported the centre, saying public health experts had called for one for decades."],against:["Coalition speakers said the bill was rushed and should have had public Senate hearings, citing Australia's high pandemic preparedness ranking.","The Coalition said broad powers to withhold advice and new FOI exemptions reduce transparency, and data-sharing declarations need independent approval.","Coalition members said moving biosecurity powers away from the Chief Medical Officer to a department head could lower public confidence.","Independents and Greens said chronic disease and climate health should be covered from day one, funding was too low, and appointments should be independent.","One Nation and some Coalition members said the centre could repeat what they described as COVID-era overreach on people's freedoms."],speeches:123,speakers:51,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-09-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28849/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28901/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_09_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28855/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_27_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28867/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28873/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28876/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_05_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7370:{overview:"This bill raises the threshold for waiving small Centrelink debts to $250 and lets debts caused by domestic violence or financial abuse be waived. It also makes a past way of calculating debts, called income apportionment, legally valid, and sets up payments of up to $600 for people affected. Much of the Senate debate focused on a schedule added late in the House, letting payments be cancelled for people evading arrest warrants for serious violent or sexual offences.",for:["Government and crossbench members said lifting and indexing the small debt waiver would clear about 1.2 million small debts and spare people stress.","Labor, Greens and independent speakers welcomed letting debts be waived where a partner's coercion or financial abuse caused them.","The government said validating income apportionment avoids recalculating millions of old debts, which would cost billions and cause distress.","The government said payment cancellation would apply only to people charged with serious violent or sexual offences who are evading police, with dependants considered.","The Coalition supported the bill and agreed with the intent of the payment cancellation schedule."],against:["The Greens and One Nation said making past unlawful debt calculations valid denies people a proper remedy, and the $600 maximum is too low.","The Coalition and the Ombudsman raised that the bill does not deal with people criminally convicted over wrongly calculated debts.","The Greens, Senator Pocock and Senator Thorpe said the late schedule cancels payments without a conviction, skipped committee review and could harm children.","Senator Pocock moved to split the late schedule into its own bill for an inquiry; the Greens sought a higher $440 waiver.","Independent MPs Wilkie and Haines moved to bring back a six-year limit on debt recovery, as the robodebt royal commission recommended."],speeches:262,speakers:44,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28852/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28855/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_27_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28898/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28870/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28876/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_05_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28882/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28885/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28888/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29201/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7371:{overview:"This bill changes the Freedom of Information Act, which lets people ask for government documents. It allows application fees for requests that are not about a person's own information, adds a 40-hour processing cap, ends fully anonymous requests, lets agencies refuse vexatious requests, and changes the rules for cabinet and advice documents. The debate focused on whether the changes fix an overloaded system or reduce government transparency.",for:["Government members said the 40-year-old law is out of date, with FOI costs rising to over $86 million and more than one million staff hours a year.","They said agencies need tools to deal with vexatious, abusive and automated requests so genuine requests can be answered faster.","The government said requests for your own personal information, about three in four requests, would stay free, with hardship waivers for fees.","Government members said cabinet confidentiality must be protected, and the bill makes clear that labelling a document 'cabinet' is not enough.","The government amended the bill so people can ask someone else to request non-personal documents on their behalf without naming them."],against:["Coalition speakers opposed the bill, saying it shifts the law away from openness and that fees would act as a tax on seeking information.","Opponents said ending anonymous requests could deter whistleblowers and vulnerable people, and that no clear evidence of bots or foreign misuse was given.","Independent members said the wider cabinet exemption goes against the robodebt royal commission and moved amendments to remove it and the fees.","Critics said the 40-hour cap, longer timeframes and new reasons to refuse could block complex public interest requests.","Crossbench members and the Coalition called for an independent, full review of the FOI system before making major changes."],speeches:133,speakers:36,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-09-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28849/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28872/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28875/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_05_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28882/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7372:{overview:"This bill moves some public health powers to the new Australian Centre for Disease Control. It lets the centre decide which human diseases are watched at the border, manage national disease data and a lung disease registry, and protects some sensitive information from freedom of information requests. The transcript includes only the Health Minister's speeches, and the bill passed the House.",for:["The Health Minister said an independent, science-based centre with strong data skills would spot health risks earlier and give better advice to government.","He said another pandemic will come, so the centre needs clear powers at the border and over national disease monitoring.","The Government said limits on freedom of information release balance the public's right to know with protecting personal and sensitive information.","The Government said repealing the old preventive health agency law clears the way for the centre to take on chronic disease work after a 2028 review."],against:[],speeches:4,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-09-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28849/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28855/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_27_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7373:{overview:"This bill requires employers, from 1 July 2026, to pay workers' super at the same time as wages, with the money reaching the fund within seven business days of payday. It also redesigns the penalty charge for late or unpaid super and helps the tax office spot missed payments sooner. Debate focused on unpaid super, the impact on small business cash flow and the start date, and super for workers under 18.",for:["The government said about $5.2 billion in super goes unpaid each year, and paying it with wages makes missed payments easier to spot and recover.","Labor speakers said earlier payments grow through compound interest, leaving a typical 25-year-old about $6,000 better off at retirement.","Government members said unpaid super hits young, low-paid, casual workers and women hardest, and stops dodgy businesses undercutting honest ones.","Labor speakers said the tax office will take a lenient approach in the first year for employers genuinely trying to comply.","The Coalition, Greens and crossbench member Rebekha Sharkie said they supported the principle of paying super on payday."],against:["Coalition speakers said Treasury advised an 18-month lead time, and moved to delay the start for businesses with fewer than 20 staff.","Coalition and One Nation senators said closing the free small business clearing house and the tight timeline would burden small businesses.","One Nation opposed the bill, arguing it brings forward costs that could lead small businesses to cut staff or close.","The Greens moved to give super to all workers under 18 regardless of hours, and later moved to disallow a regulation keeping that exclusion.","The Greens' Senate motion, which passed, called on the government to regulate super fund advertising when people start new jobs."],speeches:85,speakers:31,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28901/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_09_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28898/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28870/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28873/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29226/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7374:{overview:"This bill makes sure the superannuation guarantee charge applies for any day wages are paid and super contributions fall short. The Treasurer said it works alongside the Payday Superannuation bill to make sure super is paid on time. The transcript only covers its introduction and passage, with no debate.",for:["The Treasurer said the bill works with the Payday Superannuation bill to make sure workers' super is paid on time."],against:[],speeches:2,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28901/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_09_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7375:{overview:"This bill pauses the automatic twice-yearly rise in tax on draught beer poured from kegs in pubs and clubs for two years, from August 2025 to July 2027. The tax then resumes rising from the paused rate. Every party supported the freeze, and the debate focused on whether it goes far enough and who benefits most.",for:["The Government said the pause gives about 10,000 venues, mostly small family businesses, more certainty and helps keep the price of a pint steady.","Labor members said pubs and clubs are community hubs that employ about 160,000 people and have struggled since the pandemic.","Government speakers said the measure is targeted, temporary and affordable, and does not change public health settings or the wider alcohol tax system.","The Coalition supported the bill, noting it had taken the same freeze to the last election."],against:["Coalition speakers said the freeze saves less than one cent a pint and does nothing about wider inflation and cost-of-living pressures.","The Coalition and Nationals moved amendments to include spirits served on tap and to require a full review of alcohol tax.","Independents said the benefit mostly goes to big foreign-owned brewers that control pub taps, and moved to include bottled craft beer and spirits.","Monique Ryan and Senator Tyrrell moved to make the freeze permanent, while Barnaby Joyce and Andrew Gee moved to remove the tax on drinks in venues entirely.","In the Senate, the Greens moved a motion noting links between alcohol and family violence and calling for stronger alcohol rules."],speeches:132,speakers:67,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28869/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28881/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28884/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28890/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_27_Official%20with%20map.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29132/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29134/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29201/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29217/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7376:{overview:"This bill stops employers from cancelling or refusing employer-funded paid parental leave because a baby is stillborn or dies, unless both sides have clearly agreed otherwise. It is named after Baby Priya, whose mother's leave was cancelled after she died at six weeks old. All parties supported the main idea, but the debate also focused on whether the bill covers late-term abortions, and on the Senate cutting debate short.",for:["The Government said grieving parents need certainty and time to heal, not negotiations with their employer about returning to work.","Labor members said the bill lines up employer-paid leave with unpaid leave and the government scheme, which already continue after a baby's death.","Supporters said the bill gives managers a clear rule and does not force employers to offer paid parental leave if they do not already.","Coalition senator Anne Ruston, the Greens, David Pocock and One Nation all supported the bill's main aim.","Government, Greens and other senators said late-term terminations are rare medical decisions and that this concern was not raised by the bill."],against:["Several Coalition members backed the bill's aim but asked the government to clarify whether the stillbirth definition includes late-term abortions.","One Nation and Senator Antic moved amendments to exclude intentional terminations, and Senator Canavan moved to exempt cases protecting the mother's health.","Coalition senators criticised the government for limiting Senate debate time on a matter of conscience.","Senator McDonald said small businesses would carry extra costs and employer groups were not properly heard.","The Greens and Senator Payman supported the bill but said it should go further, such as 52 weeks of paid parental leave and reproductive health leave."],speeches:52,speakers:34,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28901/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_09_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28898/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28870/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7377:{overview:"This bill puts into law the government's freeze on the indexation of draught beer excise for two years. In debate, non-government members Pat Conaghan and Alison Penfold supported the bill but moved and backed amendments to also freeze tax on tap spirits and to push for a wider review of alcohol tax.",for:["The government said the bill strikes the right balance between supporting pubs and clubs and keeping the budget sustainable.","Speakers said they valued the role pubs and clubs play in local communities."],against:["Pat Conaghan said the freeze delivers less than a cent per pint and does not fix a broken alcohol tax system.","He argued twice-yearly automatic indexation adds to cost-of-living pressure and called for a full Treasury review of alcohol tax.","Alison Penfold backed extending the freeze to tap spirits, saying many drinkers, including many women, prefer them to beer.","The government did not support the amendments."],speeches:8,speakers:4,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29134/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7378:{overview:"This bill sets up a Parliamentary Joint Committee on Defence, modelled on the intelligence and security committee, to oversee the defence force, the Defence department, Veterans' Affairs and related agencies. It could receive classified information in private, with criminal penalties for leaks. The debate focused on who should sit on the committee, especially whether crossbench members should be included or kept out.",for:["Government members said current oversight of Defence is spread out and cannot examine classified matters, and this committee fills that gap.","Supporters said rising defence spending and projects like AUKUS need closer parliamentary scrutiny of costs and delays.","Government speakers said the bill copies a proven committee model, with strong safeguards to protect sensitive information.","The Coalition supported the bill, saying Australia's AUKUS partners already have dedicated defence committees.","Coalition members argued membership should be limited to government and opposition parties, as with the intelligence committee, to keep it bipartisan."],against:["Independent members said the bill does not guarantee crossbench seats, even though about a third of voters chose minor parties or independents.","Crossbench members moved amendments to reserve seats for crossbench or minor party members, which were not agreed to.","Senator David Pocock said he would only support the bill if crossbench representation was guaranteed.","The Greens opposed the bill, arguing a committee meeting in private with only major parties would reduce real scrutiny.","The Greens moved an amendment to make clear the committee would not limit other oversight, such as Senate estimates."],speeches:128,speakers:48,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28858/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28898/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28875/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_05_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28879/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29201/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29204/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29205/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29206/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7379:{overview:"This bill puts the Triple Zero Custodian into law and gives the communications regulator, ACMA, power to demand information from phone companies and direct them to act, with civil penalties if they do not comply. It followed the September 2025 Optus outage, when hundreds of triple 0 calls failed and lives were lost. All sides supported the bill, but the debate centred on whether it went far enough, with arguments over penalties, public reporting and an independent inquiry; in the Senate the Government accepted a Greens amendment to triple the penalties.",for:["The Government said the custodian will give one body end-to-end oversight of triple 0, as the Bean review recommended after the 2023 outage.","Government members said ACMA's new powers let it get outage details quickly and order fixes, backed by civil penalties.","Government senators said the bill was urgent and should not be delayed by inquiries or lengthy amendments before disaster season.","Government members pointed to new rules from 1 November requiring real-time outage reporting and triple 0 testing during upgrades.","Independent Helen Haines and Greens senators supported the bill as a needed step while saying more action was required."],against:["Coalition speakers said the bill was rushed, adds bureaucracy, and came 18 months after the Bean review was accepted.","The Coalition said ACMA should not investigate the outage because it was part of the failed response, and called for an independent inquiry.","The Coalition moved amendments for higher penalties, a public outage register, more frequent published reports and critical infrastructure status; these were not agreed to.","The Greens said phone company fines were too small and pushed for much higher and criminal penalties; a tripling of penalties was accepted.","Nationals member Anne Webster and Helen Haines said regional Australians face extra risks, calling for regional reporting, disaster roaming and longer tower backup power."],speeches:86,speakers:25,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-07",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28899/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_07_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28856/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_27_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28859/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7382:{overview:"This bill lets the government list foreign state entities as state sponsors of terrorism, creates new offences for dealing with them, and gives agencies the same powers they have for other terrorism. It followed ASIO's finding that Iran's Islamic Revolutionary Guard Corps directed two attacks in Australia. All parties who spoke supported it, and the debate focused on its timing, the short committee review, and protections for aid workers.",for:["The Government said state-sponsored attacks on Australian soil were a turning point and the law must adapt because current listing rules do not cover state bodies.","The Government said safeguards include the Foreign Minister's written agreement, a briefing for the Opposition Leader, and the Attorney-General's consent to prosecutions.","Coalition speakers supported the bill but said it was long overdue, as they had called for the IRGC to be listed for more than two years.","The Greens supported listing the IRGC, citing concerns raised by Iranian and Kurdish communities.","One Nation supported the bill, saying the reversed onus of proof in some defences was justified here but should always be closely scrutinised."],against:["Coalition and Greens speakers said the intelligence committee review was rushed, giving submitters only about seven days.","Coalition speaker Andrew Wallace said the bill gives no guidance on what the Foreign Minister should consider before agreeing to a listing, and flagged an oversight amendment.","The Greens moved amendments to protect humanitarian aid groups from terrorism offences; the Government and Coalition said this bill was not the right vehicle and voted against them.","The Greens argued the same test could apply to the Israel Defense Forces, and asked for no double standards in listings.","A Greens second reading amendment for Senator Thorpe called for more focus on far-right extremism; the Government opposed it."],speeches:23,speakers:13,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28858/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28898/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28873/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28876/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_05_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28879/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7385:{overview:"This bill sets up an independent Commonwealth Parole Board of experts to decide whether federal offenders are released on parole, taking that job from the Attorney-General. The debate focused on who should be accountable for parole decisions, the cost of the board, how members are chosen, and whether victims have a guaranteed say.",for:["The Government said parole decisions should be made by experts in law, policing, corrections and victim support, not by politicians.","Labor members said every state and territory, and countries like the UK, Canada and New Zealand, already use independent parole boards.","The Government said federal parole cases are rising in number and complexity, and that legal groups such as the Law Council support the change.","Government members said the rules for parole, including the very high bar for terrorism offenders, would not change.","The Greens supported the bill, saying it would depoliticise parole, and secured a change so the department secretary cannot sit on the board."],against:["The Coalition opposed the bill, saying an elected Attorney-General should stay accountable to parliament for releasing serious federal offenders.","Coalition speakers said the board would cost $28.3 million over four years and $7.3 million a year, duplicating work already done.","Coalition senators said the bill gives victims no legal right to be notified or heard, unlike several state laws, and that guidelines need not be published.","Coalition speakers objected to members reflecting the make-up of the community, saying appointments should be on merit and experience.","The Coalition moved amendments to let the Attorney-General object to parole decisions. Senator Thorpe moved amendments requiring interviews, procedural fairness and legal representation, which were defeated."],speeches:42,speakers:21,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28884/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29204/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29206/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29207/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29153/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29217/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7386:{overview:"This bill makes changes to the Crimes Act so that a new Commonwealth Parole Board, rather than the Attorney-General, makes parole decisions for federal offenders. The minister said it also lets the board delay some parole decisions, for example while housing is arranged or a treatment program is finished. No member spoke in a debate on the bill, which passed the House.",for:["The Government said the board would have the powers it needs to work effectively, including making, refusing and changing parole and licence orders.","The Government said new flexibility on deadlines would avoid decisions being made on too little information and would match best practice in states and overseas.","The Government said regular parole reviews for offenders would still be protected by law."],against:[],speeches:4,speakers:3,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28900/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_08_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28884/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7392:{overview:"This bill sets the restoration charge, a payment a developer can make into a national restoration fund instead of finding its own environmental offset. It was debated together with six other bills that rewrite national environment laws (the EPBC Act), including a new national Environment Protection Agency, national standards, a 'net gain' rule for offsets and faster approvals. The debate covered the whole package, how the offsets fund would work, and a late Senate deal between Labor and the Greens that changed rules on forestry, land clearing and fast-tracking for coal and gas.",for:["Government members said current laws fail both nature and business, as the Samuel review found, and that the package follows that review's recommendations.","The government said pooling payments in a fund run by an independent holder allows larger restoration work, such as wildlife corridors, than many small separate offsets.","Labor speakers said one streamlined assessment pathway and less overlap with the states would give quicker answers for housing, renewable energy and mining projects.","Government members said offsets must now deliver a net gain, and amendments let the minister list species too threatened to be paid for through the fund.","The Greens said their amendments ended the forestry exemption, tightened land-clearing exemptions, kept the water trigger federal and stopped coal and gas being fast-tracked."],against:["Independent members said the fund could let developers 'pay to destroy', noting similar state funds underdelivered, and moved amendments to limit it, price it properly and publish its reports.","Coalition members said offset costs already block housing and infrastructure, and an undefined 'net gain' rule and unclear 'unacceptable impact' tests would make approvals slower and costlier.","Coalition and One Nation senators argued the Labor-Greens deal threatens jobs in native forestry, farming and gas; the government said it does not shut down native forestry.","Crossbench members raised concerns about broad ministerial discretion, the national interest pathway and the EPA's independence, while Coalition members said the EPA's leader should answer to the minister.","Coalition, crossbench and Greens members criticised the short time for scrutiny, and the Greens and several independents said the laws still lack a climate trigger."],speeches:316,speakers:91,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28872/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28875/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_05_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28882/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28891/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_27_Official%20with%20map.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7393:{overview:"This bill sets up a National Environmental Protection Agency, described by the Government as Australia's first national, independent environmental regulator with new powers and penalties. It was part of a package of changes to national environment law. The short debate in this transcript focused on amendments about how the agency's leaders are chosen and how open its decisions are, and on how much time the House had for debate.",for:["The Government said the agency would be Australia's first national, independent environmental regulator, with strong new powers and penalties to protect and restore the environment.","The Government said the bill works with wider law changes to deliver stronger protections, faster and sturdier project assessments, and more trust in decisions.","The Government said its model keeps compliance and enforcement independent while the minister stays accountable for approvals.","The Government said publishing reasons for every routine decision would be an undue burden, and the reforms already publish recommendation reports for approvals.","A Labor member later said the agency, starting on 1 July 2026, answered long-standing community calls for a stronger environmental regulator."],against:["Independent member Sophie Scamps moved amendments to create an independent board that would choose the agency's CEO, saying a minister-appointed CEO would weaken trust.","Her amendments called for public advertising and an independent panel to shortlist board members, with at least one Indigenous member.","Independent member Allegra Spender moved an amendment requiring the agency to publish reasons for its decisions, not just notice of them.","The Coalition said the Government cut debate short and stopped it asking the minister questions about the bills.","The Coalition said both conservation and business groups found the bills unworkable, and raised concerns about the agency's broad powers and limits on the minister's oversight of the CEO."],speeches:24,speakers:7,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7394:{overview:"This bill sets up a framework to charge fees for some work done under Australia's main environment protection law. The minister said the actual activities and amounts would be set in regulations, limited to recovering costs, and consulted on first. The transcript shows only the minister's short introduction and the procedural votes, with no other speeches.",for:["The minister said the charges would only cover the cost of running the environment law.","The minister said any charging arrangements would be consulted on before they started."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7395:{overview:"This bill would set up a framework for general charges for certain activities linked to running the Environment Protection and Biodiversity Conservation Act. The actual activities and amounts would be set later in regulations. The recorded debate is short and mostly procedural, with the bill passing the House after the question was put without further debate.",for:["The minister said charges would be limited to what is needed to recover costs.","The minister said any charging arrangements would be consulted on before they were imposed."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7396:{overview:"This bill would set up a framework for charging fees, as excise, for certain activities linked to running the Environment Protection and Biodiversity Conservation Act. The actual activities and amounts would be set later in regulations. The recorded debate is short and mostly procedural, with the bill passing the House after the question was put without further debate.",for:["The minister said charges would be limited to what is needed to recover costs.","The minister said any charging arrangements would be consulted on before they started.","The minister argued the wider package responds to a review that had waited about five years, and that both the environment and business needed it."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7397:{overview:"This bill would create a new statutory role, the Head of Environment Information Australia, to lead work on making national environmental data easier to find and use. The minister said it would also bring independent reporting on the state of the environment. The recorded debate is short and mostly procedural, with the bill passing the House after the question was put without further debate.",for:["The minister said a 2020 independent review found Australia's environmental data was scattered and had major gaps.","The minister argued better data would support faster, better-informed decisions by government and business while protecting important environmental areas.","The minister said independent reporting would improve transparency and confidence in environmental decision-making."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7398:{overview:"This bill rewrites Australia's national environment laws, based on the Samuel review. It adds national environmental standards, a 'net gain' rule for offsets, tougher penalties, a faster assessment pathway, a national interest approval pathway and a new national Environment Protection Agency. The debate focused on whether the changes balance protecting nature with speeding up approvals for housing, energy and mining.",for:["Government members said the current laws are broken and are failing both the environment and business, as the Samuel review found.","Supporters said new standards, clearer rules on unacceptable impacts and a 'net gain' offset rule would better protect and restore nature.","Government members said a streamlined pathway and less duplication with the states would speed up approvals for housing, roads, renewables and critical minerals.","Supporters said a national EPA and higher penalties would mean breaking environmental law is no longer just a cost of doing business.","Government members said Labor had a mandate from two elections and that protecting nature and supporting jobs can go together."],against:["Coalition speakers said the bill was unbalanced, would hurt productivity and could send jobs and investment overseas.","The Coalition said nearly 1,500 pages with about three weeks to review was too rushed for stakeholders.","The Coalition opposed giving the EPA assessment roles and wanted the CEO to be more accountable to the minister.","The Coalition said emissions reporting duplicated other laws, and that 'net gain' and 'unacceptable impact' definitions were unclear.","Greens senators, citing environment groups, said the bill had loopholes, including land-clearing exemptions, that could weaken nature protections."],speeches:43,speakers:20,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28870/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28872/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28873/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28876/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_05_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28881/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28882/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28890/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_27_Official%20with%20map.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"}]},r7401:{overview:"This bill lets the Department of Home Affairs publish a public online register of employers approved to sponsor temporary skilled visa workers. The register would show each sponsor's name, ABN, postcode, number of sponsored workers and their jobs, but no details about individual workers. The Coalition opposed the bill, and the debate focused on whether the register would reduce exploitation or put honest employers at risk.",for:["Government members said the register lets migrant workers check that an employer is a real approved sponsor.","Labor speakers said listing postcodes helps workers find a new sponsor nearby if they need to leave a bad employer.","Government members said underpaying migrants pulls down wages for all workers and hurts employers who do the right thing.","Supporters said the register came from the migration review and strategy, and that a Senate committee found most submissions supported it."],against:["The Coalition said publishing how many sponsored workers each business has could expose honest employers to union pressure or targeted campaigns.","Coalition members said workers can already check sponsors through migration agents, and a register of penalised employers already exists.","Coalition speakers said too much was left to regulations, with no clear process to fix errors.","The Coalition said the government should first deliver promised criminal penalties, whistleblower visas and bans on repeat offenders.","Regional Coalition members said the bill could make small and farm businesses wary of sponsoring workers."],speeches:33,speakers:23,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28890/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_27_Official%20with%20map.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29217/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7402:{overview:"This bill creates an 'orphan works' scheme, so libraries, museums and others can use material whose copyright owner cannot be found, with limits on what they can be sued for. It also makes clear that copyright rules for classes apply the same way online as in person, and that parents and community members can help with lessons. The Senate debate focused on recording lessons for later viewing and on copyright and artificial intelligence.",for:["The government said the orphan works scheme lowers legal risk for good-faith users while still letting owners who come forward ask for reasonable payment.","Coalition, Greens and One Nation senators all supported the bill, saying it opens up historical and cultural collections for researchers, educators and institutions.","Supporters said it gives teachers, students and families clarity that classroom copyright rules cover online and hybrid lessons.","The government said the bill reflects years of work with stakeholders and has broad support, and a Senate committee recommended it pass."],against:["The Greens moved an amendment asking the government to also cover recorded lessons, so students who are sick, disabled or working can catch up later.","The government opposed that amendment, saying rights holders strongly object to covering recordings and the issue needs more careful work.","Senator David Pocock moved an amendment backing fair payment for creators and rejecting any rule letting big tech use copyrighted work for AI without permission.","The Coalition and One Nation said they would watch for unintended effects, such as weak protection for creators or small businesses facing unfair claims."],speeches:15,speakers:9,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29201/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29216/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_31.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7404:{overview:"This bill requires streaming services with at least one million Australian subscribers to spend 10 per cent of their Australian program spending, or 7.5 per cent of their Australian revenue, on new Australian drama, children's, documentary, arts and educational programs. The debate focused on whether the level was high enough, how spending would be counted, and whether children's and documentary content and regional areas would be protected.",for:["The Government said streaming services, unlike free-to-air and pay TV, have no local content rules, and Australians should see their own stories on every screen.","Government members said the rules would support tens of thousands of screen industry jobs and give producers a steady pipeline of work.","The Government said a three-year averaging period and a four-year review give flexibility, and the design keeps within trade obligations.","The Coalition said it supports Australian content and the screen industry, and that it first proposed a streaming content guarantee in government.","The Greens and several independents said they supported the bill as a long-awaited step."],against:["Independents including Monique Ryan and Allegra Spender said the levels were too low and mainly lock in current spending, and called for 20 per cent.","Allegra Spender moved amendments to stop taxpayer-funded producer offsets counting as streamer spending and to require reporting by genre.","Zali Steggall moved an amendment to require balanced investment across genres, warning children's and documentary content could decline.","The Coalition said the bill was rushed and moved amendments on making Australian content easy to find and on reporting regional spending.","The Government rejected all amendments, saying the bill had been carefully drafted to comply with trade law."],speeches:47,speakers:28,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-06",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28878/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_06_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28881/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28884/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28888/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7406:{overview:"This bill lets specially trained and endorsed registered nurses prescribe certain medicines under the Pharmaceutical Benefits Scheme, so patients pay the subsidised price. These nurses must work under a prescribing agreement with a doctor or nurse practitioner, and their prescribing is subject to peer review. The debate focused on access to care in regional areas, patient safety safeguards, and whether podiatrists should be included too.",for:["Government members said nurses are highly trained but underused, and letting them prescribe will cut waits for GP visits and take pressure off emergency departments.","Speakers said this will help people in rural and remote areas, where nurses are often the only health workers nearby.","Supporters said the model was developed through years of consultation since 2017 and has safeguards, including training, mentoring and oversight.","The government said nurses can already prescribe under the new standard, so the bill makes those medicines affordable for patients.","The Coalition, Greens, One Nation and most crossbench members supported the bill."],against:["The Nationals said the model is untested and moved to require a state trial first, adding there is no funding model for primary care.","Independent Sophie Scamps, citing doctors' groups, moved to bar nurse prescribers from prescribing addictive Schedule 8 drugs and require prescription monitoring.","Senator Alex Antic opposed the bill, citing risks of fragmented care, missed diagnoses and added liability for nurses.","The Senate voted to also let podiatrists prescribe under the scheme. The government rejected this, saying that work is unfinished, and the Senate backed down.","The Coalition said the government has not formally responded to its own scope of practice review and its approach is piecemeal."],speeches:87,speakers:55,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29132/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29134/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29201/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29217/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29226/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29170/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29227/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29228/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7407:{overview:"This bill sets up the Australian Tertiary Education Commission (ATEC), a key idea from the Universities Accord review. ATEC would guide the university and TAFE system, make agreements with each university about its role and student numbers, advise ministers, and publish a yearly report on the sector. Debate focused on whether ATEC is needed, how independent it would be, its legal objective, and whether it should look at student fees.",for:["The government said Australia needs about 80 per cent of workers to hold a qualification by 2050, and ATEC will steer the reforms needed to get there.","Labor members said ATEC will help students from poorer families, the outer suburbs and the regions get into and finish university.","Supporters said ATEC will join up TAFE and university so students can move more easily between them.","The government said ministers cannot direct ATEC's advice and it will be reviewed after two and five years. In the Senate, it accepted changes on independence and research."],against:["The Coalition opposed the bill, saying it adds another regulator to an overregulated sector and costs $54 million without creating any student places.","The Coalition said the legal objective leaves out teaching, learning and research, and moved to replace it. The government did not accept this.","Crossbench members and the Greens said ATEC was not independent enough, as it could mostly only advise when the minister asked.","Crossbench members and the Greens said ATEC should be able to look at student fees and fix the Job-ready Graduates scheme. These amendments did not pass.","Nationals members said the commission should include a regional education commissioner, as the Accord recommended."],speeches:109,speakers:52,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29136/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_09.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29137/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29205/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29215/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29152/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_31.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7408:{overview:"This bill makes the related legal changes needed to set up the Australian Tertiary Education Commission, working alongside a main bill. The minister introduced it with a short speech pointing to the explanatory memorandum, and it passed the House without recorded debate.",for:["The Government said the bill is needed to put the measures in the main Australian Tertiary Education Commission bill into effect."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29137/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7409:{overview:"This bill bans life insurers from using a person's genetic test results to decide whether to offer cover or on what terms, with penalties enforced by ASIC. It also eases licensing for some foreign financial firms, updates how Australia funds the World Bank and similar banks, and scraps a planned second registration step for financial advisers. Almost all speakers supported the genetic testing ban, and debate focused on delays and on possible gaps in the protection.",for:["Government members said fear of insurance discrimination stops people having genetic tests or joining research, citing a Monash University study where many people withdrew for this reason.","Supporters said insurers can still use symptoms, diagnoses and family history, so insurance can still be priced on risk.","The Coalition supported the bill, saying the reforms had broad agreement for years and should have come sooner.","Supporters said the other schedules cut unneeded red tape for financial advisers and give investors access to global financial services.","Independents and Labor members said a law replaces an industry-run moratorium that had not given people certainty."],against:["The Coalition said the new funding rules for development banks allow standing spending with no clear upper limit, which reduces parliament's scrutiny.","Independents Monique Ryan and Sophie Scamps said the ban does not remove unfair terms already placed on existing policies.","Monique Ryan moved an amendment for reviews every three years instead of five, which the Government opposed as too soon.","Sophie Scamps moved amendments, based on Human Rights Commission concerns, to stop regulations narrowing what counts as protected genetic information. The Government opposed them.","Some speakers warned insurers might guess genetic results from other medical information."],speeches:78,speakers:42,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29147/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_23.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29148/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29149/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29217/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7411:{overview:"This bill brings businesses that hold digital assets, such as crypto, for customers under Australia's financial services laws. It creates two new financial products, digital asset platforms and tokenised custody platforms, which will need a licence and must meet ASIC standards on custody and disclosure, with an exemption for small operators and an 18-month transition. Speakers from all sides backed the bill, and the debate focused on consumer protection, how long the reform had taken, and what it left out.",for:["The Government said businesses can currently hold unlimited client digital assets with no financial law safeguards, and pointed to the FTX collapse and crypto scams.","Government members said the bill applies a simple principle: if you hold assets for clients, you meet the same standards, whether dollars or digital.","Coalition speakers welcomed the legal clarity, saying uncertainty and ASIC's court-based enforcement had driven investment and jobs offshore.","The Greens and independent Allegra Spender supported the bill, saying it closes regulatory gaps and gives honest firms certainty.","Supporters said using the existing licence system avoids a new regime and lowers compliance costs."],against:["Coalition speakers said the reform took too long and Australia had fallen behind places like the UK, EU and US.","Coalition MP Simon Kennedy said the bill does not address debanking, stablecoin payments, broker-model platforms or group licensing.","Coalition MP Tim Wilson questioned whether ASIC could do the job and said the Coalition wanted a committee inquiry.","Allegra Spender urged recognition of overseas crypto rules and said 'pump and dump' token schemes would fall outside the new rules.","In the Senate, Coalition amendments sought a review after two years and required banks to give reasons for refusing service; Senator Pocock backed the review."],speeches:25,speakers:20,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29132/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29134/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29201/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29217/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7412:{overview:"This bill lets employers find a new worker's existing super fund earlier when they start a job, and bans most super advertising during job onboarding, with some exceptions. It also gives tax breaks for hosting the Rugby World Cups, enacts a tax treaty with Portugal, lists charities for tax-deductible donations and raises the wine tax rebate cap to $400,000. Debate focused on whether the advertising ban helps or limits choice, and on charity status for Equality Australia.",for:["Government members said showing workers their existing fund at the start of a job reduces duplicate accounts and the extra fees and insurance they bring.","Labor speakers said a review found some onboarding software steered new workers into advertised funds linked to the provider, which the ban will stop.","Government members said workers can still choose any fund, and approved MySuper products that pass the performance test can still be shown.","The Greens supported the bill and the advertising limits, and backed charity status for Equality Australia.","Most speakers, including the Coalition, supported the Rugby World Cup exemptions, the Portugal treaty and the higher wine rebate cap."],against:["Coalition speakers said the advertising ban limits choice and competition, and argued it favours industry super funds.","Coalition senators cited evidence that the tax office finds existing funds only 55 to 80 per cent of the time, which could leave workers seeing only the default fund.","Independent Allegra Spender said advertising helps new funds compete, and asked for a review of the impact of super rules on competition.","Coalition and One Nation senators moved to remove Equality Australia's listing, noting it had been refused public benevolent institution status by the regulator and courts.","Coalition members said unrelated measures were bundled together; the Greens unsuccessfully moved to widen charity rules for animal welfare and remove status for groups supporting illegal occupations."],speeches:118,speakers:54,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29138/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29139/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29140/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29205/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29209/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29211/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_23.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7414:{overview:"This bill creates a Universal Outdoor Mobile Obligation, requiring Telstra, Optus and TPG to make basic mobile voice and text coverage reasonably available outdoors across Australia, from a default date of 1 December 2027. Coverage is expected to come from existing towers plus new satellite-to-phone technology, and the minister can adjust timing, scope and exemptions. Debate focused on whether the obligation is clear and enforceable, handset compatibility, affordability and triple zero access.",for:["Government members said towers cover where 99 per cent of people live but only a third of the land, leaving huge areas where triple zero calls are impossible.","Labor speakers said the obligation could add about five million square kilometres of outdoor coverage, helping people in disasters, on remote roads and on farms.","The government said legislating now pushes carriers to roll out satellite coverage quickly, rather than leaving it to commercial decisions alone.","Government members said the minister can set enforceable standards, including on affordability, and voice services will be covered by existing triple zero rules.","Independents and the Greens supported the bill, and the government accepted an amendment from Helen Haines requiring reasons for any exemption decisions."],against:["Coalition speakers said terms like 'reasonably available' and 'equitable basis' are undefined, and the start date can be delayed, making the obligation hard to enforce.","Coalition and crossbench members said satellite voice is not yet available at scale and many older or cheaper phones cannot connect, risking a repeat of 3G shutdown problems.","Coalition members called for a Senate inquiry and argued the bill should explicitly require triple zero access, including texting triple zero.","Independent Helen Haines moved amendments defining affordability and allowing disaster roaming rules; most were rejected as the government said existing powers were enough.","Nationals members said the bill should also cover maintaining existing mobile towers and introduce mandatory roaming between networks."],speeches:74,speakers:48,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28890/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_27_Official%20with%20map.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29140/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29145/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29160/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_28.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29172/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29181/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_09.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7415:{overview:"This bill creates a new law that sets out the jobs of the Translating and Interpreting Service (TIS National) in the Department of Home Affairs. Speakers said it does not change how the service works or is funded, but gives it a clear legal basis after government legal advice. Both major parties supported it, and the debate also covered English language skills and multiculturalism.",for:["Government members said TIS National helps people with limited English deal with doctors, courts, police and emergency services.","The Coalition supported the bill as technical, saying it fixes an Auditor-General finding about the legal basis for charging fees.","Government speakers said a clear law reduces legal risk, which they linked to lessons from the robodebt royal commission.","Several members said professional interpreters mean children no longer have to interpret for their parents in sensitive situations."],against:["Barnaby Joyce did not oppose the service but argued the main focus should be on migrants learning English, and called for English rules for public servants.","The Greens moved Senate motions calling for a core TIS workforce of public servants, more funding for interpreter accreditation, and First Nations-led interpreting services.","Australia's Voice proposed changes on interpreter pay, job security, qualifications and a national language access framework."],speeches:24,speakers:18,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28887/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29134/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29217/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7417:{overview:"This bill makes technical changes so veterans' compensation claims can move to a single law, the Military Rehabilitation and Compensation Act, from 1 July 2026. It covers things like funeral payments, payments for partners after a service death, education help for children and gold cards. Both sides supported it, but the debate focused on rules for telling the Chief of the Defence Force when a serving member seeks review or health care.",for:["The government said the changes are needed so the move to one simpler law is smooth and no veteran gets lower payments.","Government members said a single act will make entitlements easier to understand and claims faster to process.","The Coalition said it supports the bill because simplifying the old three-act system follows the royal commission's advice."],against:["Coalition members opposed telling the Chief of the Defence Force when a serving member goes to the Veterans' Review Board or uses non-liability health care.","Phillip Thompson said this could make members afraid to seek mental health help and could harm their careers.","Coalition speakers said this was the second fix-up bill and the work had taken too long.","The government then moved its own amendment to remove the power to notify the Chief about non-liability health care."],speeches:16,speakers:12,days:[{house:"House",date:"2025-11-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28890/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_27_Official%20with%20map.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29132/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29134/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7420:{overview:"This transcript does not include debate on the bill itself. It records a parliamentary intelligence and security committee tabling its review of a draft version of the bill, which included a dissenting report. It also records Senator Payman moving motions asking the government to table documents about how this bill and two related laws were developed.",for:[],against:["Senator Payman moved motions seeking emails, briefings and meeting notes between ministers and their departments about how the bill was developed in December 2025 and January 2026."],speeches:5,speakers:3,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-01-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29340/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_01_20_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29211/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_23.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7421:{overview:"This bill was a response to the December 2025 Bondi terrorist attack, in which 15 people were killed. It sets up a framework for a national gun buyback, lets ASIO and criminal intelligence inform state firearm licence decisions, tightens gun import rules, creates offences for sharing 3D-printed gun blueprints online, and bans imports of extremist material. Labor and the Greens supported it, while the Coalition and One Nation opposed it, and the debate focused on whether gun laws were the right response and how fast the bill was passed.",for:["The Government said the attackers used legally held guns, and under these changes they would have held none, since one was not a citizen.","Government and Greens speakers said Australia now has over four million registered guns, more than before Port Arthur, and about 2,000 are stolen each year.","Supporters said sharing ASIO and criminal intelligence with licensing authorities closes a gap, a measure many opponents also backed.","Labor and Greens senators said fewer guns and tighter import rules also lower risks from domestic violence and attacks on police.","The Greens said the Government agreed in writing to set up a national firearms safety council to keep gun laws up to date."],against:["Coalition and One Nation speakers said the attack was caused by Islamist extremism, not lawful gun owners, and the bill unfairly targets farmers and sporting shooters.","Opponents said the bill was rushed, with little consultation and a short inquiry that received over 7,000 submissions.","The Coalition said the buyback lacks fair market value pay and business compensation like 1996, and some states have not agreed to it.","Opponents said criminals will not hand in guns, and limits on online material could catch lawful owners reading manuals or reloading guides.","Independents in both houses backed parts of the bill but moved amendments for domestic violence checks, a later inquiry, a review and fair compensation."],speeches:110,speakers:52,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-01-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29340/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_01_20_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-01-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29342/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_01_20_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29205/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29206/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7422:{overview:"Introduced after the December 2025 Bondi terrorist attack, this bill raises penalties for threatening violence against groups, creates tougher offences for religious leaders who preach violence and adults who radicalise children, and makes racial hatred count against offenders at sentencing. It lets the government list and ban 'prohibited hate groups' and widens powers to refuse or cancel visas for spreading hatred. A planned racial vilification offence was removed before debate, and the debate focused on that removal, the rushed process, and safeguards.",for:["The government said violent extremism starts with words, and the bill targets people and groups who spread hate before violence happens.","Coalition speakers supported the bill after negotiating changes, saying it closes a gap that let groups like Hizb ut-Tahrir and neo-Nazi groups operate legally.","Supporters said hate group listings require high thresholds, ASIO advice and a parliamentary disallowance process, plus a review after two years.","The Coalition said changing the visa test from 'would' to 'might' pose a risk was reasonable to protect Australians.","Labor members said the bill was an important step, but argued the Coalition should have supported the removed vilification laws."],against:["Many crossbench members, the Greens and some Coalition members said the bill was rushed, with little time to read it and a very short committee inquiry.","The Greens opposed the bill, saying it could chill protest and political speech, especially about Palestine, and unfairly targets migrants and Muslims.","Crossbench members said protections should cover religion, sexuality, gender and disability too. Their amendments for this did not pass.","Critics said the hate group listing power lacks procedural fairness and merits review. Amendments for review, sunset clauses and parliamentary approval failed.","The Nationals and One Nation sought a Senate inquiry, and Barnaby Joyce opposed the bill for relying on state definitions of hate crimes."],speeches:98,speakers:48,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-01-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29340/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_01_20_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-01-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29342/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_01_20_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29205/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29206/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29214/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7423:{overview:"This bill widens the legal meaning of 'vaccine' so that products giving ready-made antibodies, known as passive immunity, can be listed on the free National Immunisation Program. Speakers focused on Beyfortus, which protects babies from RSV, and on wider issues of falling vaccination rates and uneven vaccine funding between states.",for:["The Government said new therapies like Beyfortus cannot be listed under the current definition, so Australians could miss out on products available overseas.","Coalition speakers supported the bill as a sensible technical change that gives families nationally consistent access to RSV protection for babies.","The Coalition noted the bill does not list any product automatically; products must still pass the usual safety, effectiveness and value assessments.","Independent member Monique Ryan supported the bill, saying it would help protect against preventable diseases and make the program more flexible."],against:["Monique Ryan moved an amendment urging the Government to work with states on uneven vaccine funding, such as for meningococcal B and RSV. It was defeated.","Coalition speakers backed the bill but said medicines take too long to be listed and the Government had not acted on a health technology assessment review.","One Nation moved amendments in the Senate requiring vaccines to be tested against a saline placebo to an international standard before listing."],speeches:11,speakers:8,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29137/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29138/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29139/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29217/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7424:{overview:"This bill makes technical changes to child support and social security law. It gives parents more time before a new child support assessment starts, confirms that people with less than 35 per cent care of a child cannot receive child support, and gives a clear legal basis for urgent payments and for counting a partner's income. The Coalition did not oppose it, the Greens supported it but moved amendments, and much of the debate was about urgent payments and wider welfare issues.",for:["The government said the bill gives clear legal backing to long-standing practices and fixes errors from earlier law changes.","Government members said parents will get a month to prepare for a new child support amount instead of just days.","The government said removing the two-a-year limit on urgent payments comes with safeguards and offers of financial counselling.","The Coalition said it supports practical steps that improve how the child support scheme works."],against:["Coalition speakers worried that removing the yearly cap on urgent payments could lead to more reliance on them and deeper money stress.","Zali Steggall, member for Warringah, said the bill was too small and ignored calls to fix how child support can be used for financial abuse.","The Greens said the bill fixes practices that had no legal basis for years, and moved to remove or index the $200 urgent payment limit.","The Greens also moved to bring back a six-year limit on recovering welfare debts and to raise payments above the poverty line.","The government opposed these amendments, saying the bill is about legal certainty, not payment amounts."],speeches:42,speakers:12,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29137/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29138/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29139/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29214/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29217/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7425:{overview:"This bill makes the National Commission for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Children and Young People a permanent, independent statutory agency. The commissioner could hold inquiries, require information, report to parliament and hear directly from children. The debate focused on whether a new national body would improve outcomes or duplicate existing roles, and whether governments should have to respond to its recommendations.",for:["Government members said Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander children are far more likely to be in out-of-home care and youth detention, and a dedicated national voice is needed.","Supporters said more than 70 organisations, including SNAICC, had called for a legislated commissioner for decades.","Government members said the commissioner would coordinate with existing commissioners through referrals rather than duplicate their work.","Supporters said legislation gives the commissioner powers to hold inquiries, gather information and report to parliament, which interim arrangements lacked.","Independent members supported the bill, while urging the government to respond to reports and raise the age of criminal responsibility."],against:["Coalition speakers said the commission duplicates existing national, state and territory commissioners and adds Canberra-based bureaucracy.","The Coalition argued the $33.5 million would be better spent on frontline services such as school attendance and child protection.","Coalition members said the bill shifts accountability away from ministers and that consultation for it was too narrow.","The Greens supported the bill but moved an amendment requiring the government to formally respond to the commissioner's recommendations.","One Nation and Senator Jacinta Nampijinpa Price opposed the bill as divisive by race, adding bureaucracy without fixing core problems."],speeches:65,speakers:42,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29137/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29138/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29205/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29211/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_23.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29213/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7426:{overview:"This bill moves the National Commission for Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Children and Young People, set up as an executive agency in January 2025, and its current commissioner into a permanent statutory agency and office. It is one of a package of two bills. The transcript only covers the minister's introduction and the bill passing the House, with no debate recorded.",for:[],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29138/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7428:{overview:"This bill provides an extra $9.2 million for the Department of the House of Representatives and the Department of Parliamentary Services for the rest of 2025-26. It was debated together with two larger spending bills, worth about $12.7 billion in total. Most speeches were about the government's overall spending, inflation and local projects, rather than the parliamentary departments themselves.",for:["Government members said the bills fund decisions already made, including cheaper home batteries, Medicare, the NDIS, defence and social housing.","Labor speakers pointed to more bulk-billing, $25 medicine scripts, urgent care clinics, tax cuts and student debt cuts as cost-of-living help.","A Labor member said funding parliament properly helps members represent constituents and lets committees do their work.","The Coalition said it would not block or delay the bills, as giving the government money to run is normal practice."],against:["Coalition speakers said government spending is too high and is adding to inflation and interest rate rises, and pointed to debt nearing $1 trillion.","Regional Coalition and Nationals members said their areas were missing out on roads, urgent care clinics, mobile towers and other funding.","Crossbench members suggested changes such as gas tax reform, fuel tax credit reform and more focus on prevention spending.","One Nation moved to delay the bills until all net zero funding was removed. Senator Payman raised concerns about waste on contractors and advisory bodies."],speeches:126,speakers:78,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-02-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29139/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29141/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29142/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29144/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29145/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29146/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29148/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29213/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29214/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7429:{overview:"This bill approves about $3.5 billion in extra government spending for the 2025-26 financial year. The minister said it includes over $2 billion for Defence, $411 million mainly for water buybacks in the Murray-Darling Basin, and over $325 million in loans for social and affordable housing. The transcript only covers the minister's speech and the final vote, with no debate recorded.",for:["The minister said the Defence money would help deliver priorities set out in the 2024 National Defence Strategy.","The minister said the funding would support water recovery targets and social and affordable housing projects."],against:[],speeches:2,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29148/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7430:{overview:"This bill approves about $9.2 billion of extra government spending for 2025-26, covering decisions made since the budget. The minister said it includes money for the Cheaper Home Batteries Program, the NDIS, Medicare, Defence and Home Affairs. The transcript only contains the minister's opening speech and the final vote, with no debate.",for:["The Government said the bill funds measures announced since the budget and covers changes in the cost of demand-driven programs.","The Government highlighted funding for home batteries, disability and health programs, defence plans and the response to the Bondi terrorist attack."],against:[],speeches:2,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29135/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29148/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7434:{overview:"This bill lets the Health Department publish what individual specialists charge and likely out-of-pocket costs on the Medical Costs Finder website, using billing data the government already holds. It also stops 'product phoenixing' by requiring insurers to get the minister's approval for prices on new products and for cuts to existing cover. Speakers broadly agreed with the aims, and debate focused on whether the published data would be accurate and fair and whether the bill does enough to lower costs.",for:["The Government said over 800,000 people delayed or missed specialist care in 2024-25 because of cost, and patients should know prices before committing to treatment.","Government members said the voluntary website failed, with fewer than 100 specialists choosing to publish their fees, so using existing data fixes this without extra work for doctors.","Supporters said phoenixing let insurers close a policy and relaunch a near-identical one at a higher price, avoiding the minister's annual review.","The Greens, the AMA and Private Healthcare Australia were cited as supporting the bill as a step in the right direction.","In the Senate, the Government accepted Coalition amendments to cap application fees at cost and require yearly reports on fee trends and phoenixing."],against:["The Coalition did not oppose the bill but said it was not ready, questioning how current and accurate the data would be and how errors would be fixed.","The Coalition opposed the department being protected from being sued over published fees and said approval for every new extras product was too broad.","Independents Monique Ryan, Kate Chaney and Sophie Scamps said transparency alone will not lower costs, calling for Medicare rebate indexing, quality data and an independent private health regulator.","Allegra Spender and the Coalition called for set deadlines for ministers to decide on new insurance products, which the Government did not accept.","The Greens said public money should go to public health rather than subsidising private insurance, and that dental care should be added to Medicare."],speeches:106,speakers:52,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29139/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29149/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29166/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29167/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29225/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29240/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-15",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29241/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_15.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-16",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29185/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_16.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7435:{overview:"This bill, debated together with its companion bill, raises the tax rate on super earnings to 30 per cent for balances between $3 million and $10 million and 40 per cent above $10 million, using realised earnings and indexed thresholds. It also lifts the low-income super tax offset to a maximum of $810 for people earning up to $45,000. Debate focused on fairness and budget sustainability, the government's earlier plan to tax unrealised gains, and trust in the super system.",for:["Government members said super tax concessions cost over $60 billion a year, with much of the benefit going to top income earners.","Labor speakers said the change affects fewer than 0.5 per cent of people, while 14 times as many low-income workers, mostly women, gain from the bigger offset.","Government members said rates remain below the top income tax rate, and earnings on balances under $3 million are unchanged.","Independents including Kate Chaney, Allegra Spender, Monique Ryan and David Pocock supported the revised bill after unrealised gains were dropped and thresholds indexed.","The Greens supported the bill as a small step, while saying wider tax reform is needed to address inequality between generations."],against:["Coalition speakers said the tax was not taken to the last election and breaks trust that super rules will stay stable.","Coalition members said the government has a spending problem, and warned the thresholds could later be lowered or other assets targeted.","Coalition speakers raised concerns about removing an effective exemption on death, and the effect on surviving spouses and disability benefit recipients.","Coalition members said the offset increase is welcome but does not ease cost-of-living pressures today.","Independent David Pocock raised concerns about defined benefit members, and he and others objected to the lack of a Senate inquiry and a shortened debate."],speeches:93,speakers:63,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29138/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29141/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29142/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29143/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29208/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7436:{overview:"This bill merges three bodies that set accounting, auditing and sustainability reporting standards into one new body called External Reporting Australia. It would be led by a governing council, with expert boards underneath, including a new board for sustainability standards. The debate focused on whether the change was needed, how independent the new body would be, and links to big audit firms.",for:["Government members said a single body will be more flexible and better able to respond to changes in reporting standards in Australia and overseas.","Labor speakers said public meetings, conflict-of-interest rules and a cost-benefit test for each new standard will build trust in markets.","Government members pointed to past corporate collapses and a parliamentary inquiry that recommended merging the bodies.","The Greens supported the merger and secured amendments to keep current big four partners off the new body, add ethics expertise and require a public interest test."],against:["Coalition speakers said the current system is respected and has not failed, so there is no case to abolish three bodies.","The Coalition said merging oversight and standard setting into one minister-appointed body risks political influence over technical standards.","Coalition members said the government should focus on insurance costs, bank closures and financial advice reforms instead.","Some Coalition senators said the bill could push climate and sustainability reporting onto business, and that the Greens' amendment would unfairly exclude skilled accountants.","The Greens said the bill still lets a New Zealand appointee tied to a big four firm sit on the body, and urged wider reform of audit firms."],speeches:35,speakers:23,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29139/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29141/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29142/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29143/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29209/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29225/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29169/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7437:{overview:"This bill raises the tax rate on earnings from superannuation balances above $3 million, to 30 per cent, and to 40 per cent above $10 million, with both thresholds indexed. It also boosts the low-income superannuation tax offset, lifting the maximum payment to $810 and the income limit to $45,000. The debate focused on fairness, trust in tax promises, and whether existing balances should be protected from the change.",for:["The government said super tax breaks cost over $60 billion a year and mostly help top earners, so targeting large balances is fairer and more sustainable.","Labor members said fewer than half a per cent of Australians would pay more, while about 1.3 million low-income workers, mostly women, would get more super.","Supporters said the extra tax only applies to the share of earnings above each threshold and only to future earnings.","Government members said the design reflected more than two years of consultation and changes announced in October 2025.","Independent Allegra Spender supported the bill, saying it addresses fairness between generations while being fair overall to existing super holders."],against:["The Coalition said this is a new tax not taken to the election, and that the government cannot be trusted on tax.","Coalition speakers said the government has a spending problem, and raised concerns about self-managed super funds, surviving spouses and disability benefit recipients.","The Coalition said the offset increase was welcome but modest and would not help with cost-of-living pressures now.","Coalition member Terry Young moved amendments to protect existing balances, saying it is unfair to change rules after people have planned their retirement. The government opposed this.","Allegra Spender moved an amendment allowing a one-off withdrawal for people under 60 above the threshold. The government opposed it, citing constitutional issues."],speeches:51,speakers:16,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29138/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29141/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29143/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29209/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7438:{overview:"This bill changes the rules for the heads of four Commonwealth bodies: ACIAR, Austrade, the Australian Safeguards and Non-Proliferation Office, and the Office of Parliamentary Counsel. It cuts most terms to five years, adds new reasons to sack or suspend office holders, lets ministers set performance standards, and lets the Attorney-General give general directions to the law-drafting office. The debate focused on accountability at work versus the balance of power between ministers and parliament.",for:["The Government said office holders should be removable for serious misconduct, such as bullying, harassment, discrimination or corruption, just like most working Australians.","Government members said current rules give few options when conduct or performance falls short, and the bill brings them up to modern workplace standards.","The Government said the nuclear safeguards director is treated differently, with no poor-performance sacking ground, to protect its independence.","Labor members said normal procedural fairness still applies, and suspensions beyond three months require consultation, with a 12-month limit.","Independent member Allegra Spender called the bill unobjectionable, though she asked whether it was the best use of parliament's time."],against:["The Coalition said the bill shifts power from parliament to ministers, who could set standards, judge performance, suspend and sack office holders.","Coalition senators said performance standards need not be tabled, published or open to disallowance, and the bill does not define unsatisfactory performance.","The Coalition asked why only these four bodies were covered and opposed the bill in the Senate after a committee inquiry was refused.","Coalition speakers said the new directions power over the law-drafting office and suspension power over the nuclear regulator could affect their independence.","One Nation Senator Malcolm Roberts said the bill could let ministers pressure senior public servants, and called parts of its wording sloppy."],speeches:167,speakers:12,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-02-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29138/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_02_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29141/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29142/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29206/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29211/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_23.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29212/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7443:{overview:"This bill sets up a way for people to give intelligence and sensitive police information to royal commissions, including the Royal Commission on Antisemitism and Social Cohesion, without breaking secrecy laws. It also adds a new defence in the Criminal Code for people who share information with any royal commission. The debate focused on whether the protections go far enough, how much scrutiny the bill got, and how the current royal commission is being run.",for:["The Government said secrecy laws can scare off witnesses, and the bill gives a clear, lawful path to share sensitive information with royal commissions.","The Government said the protections are targeted, follow published arrangements, and match defences that already exist for courts and integrity agencies.","The Coalition supported the bill, saying people who come forward about intelligence agencies need legal certainty about their protections.","Independent member Allegra Spender supported the bill as needed and timely, building on past reviews that found secrecy laws could deter witnesses.","A parliamentary intelligence committee said the bill strikes the right balance and recommended it pass as soon as possible."],against:["The Coalition said the bill changes national security law and needed closer review, and said the Government had rushed the intelligence committee's work.","Ms Spender said the bill could let agencies decline to share information and may not fully protect people who come forward voluntarily or face workplace payback.","The Greens said the bill only covers a few of hundreds of secrecy laws and gives security agency heads a veto over what is shared.","The Greens backed a Lambie amendment to let royal commissions use parliamentary committee material; the Government opposed it to protect parliamentary privilege.","The Greens voted against the final bill after that amendment failed, saying the bill did not give witnesses enough protection."],speeches:18,speakers:10,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-03-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29140/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29141/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29205/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29146/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29210/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7445:{overview:"This bill lets the Northern Australia Infrastructure Facility, which gives low-cost loans for projects in northern Australia, keep making investment decisions for ten more years, until 2036. It also adds the Finance Minister as a responsible minister, makes the board report when it breaks its investment rules, and sets up reviews in 2029 and 2034. The debate focused on the facility's record, whether it should be permanent, and whether it should be allowed to fund coal and gas.",for:["Government members said the facility has backed over $4 billion in loans, supporting projects forecast to create more than 18,000 jobs across the north.","Speakers pointed to projects like rare earths mining, seniors housing in Cairns, a urea fertiliser plant and a cotton gin as examples of its value.","The government said a ten-year extension gives investors certainty and the new rules improve accountability.","The Coalition and One Nation supported the extension, saying northern Australia faces high costs and the facility fills a finance gap.","Government members said rules already require projects to have plans for engaging First Nations people."],against:["The Coalition said ten years was not enough and moved to make the facility permanent, as the independent review recommended.","The Greens opposed the bill unless it was changed to ban funding for coal and gas extraction, gas pipelines and native forest logging.","Independent member Zali Steggall moved amendments to require funding to match climate laws, ban gas funding and require consultation with First Nations people.","Critics of gas funding said profitable fossil fuel companies should not get public loans and that this shifts risk onto taxpayers.","One Nation said the facility's rules should not include net zero or First Nations goals, and moved that it encourage coal, gas and mining projects."],speeches:70,speakers:28,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-03-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29142/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29144/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29145/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29210/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29225/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29226/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7447:{overview:"This bill lets the Home Affairs Minister, with written agreement from the Prime Minister and Foreign Minister, stop groups of offshore temporary visa holders from travelling to Australia for up to six months after an overseas event such as a conflict. The debate took place during the conflict involving Iran and focused on how broad the power is, what safeguards apply, and how quickly the bill passed. Labor and the Coalition supported it, while the Greens and several crossbench members opposed it or moved amendments that were not agreed to.",for:["The Government said a temporary visa means a temporary visit, and it needs a way to respond when large numbers of people may not intend to leave.","The Government said the only current option is cancelling visas one by one, which is slow and harsher than pausing travel.","Government members pointed to safeguards: a six-month limit, no effect on permanent visas or people already here, family exemptions, case-by-case exemptions and tabling in Parliament.","The Government said adding extra humanitarian places for each paused visa would be unworkable and disrupt planned settlement services.","Coalition speakers supported the bill as protecting border control, but asked why similar powers were not sought during the Gaza conflict."],against:["Independents including Kate Chaney, Monique Ryan and Zali Steggall said the test was too low, the trigger event undefined, and decisions should be disallowable by Parliament.","Crossbench members said the bill was rushed, with little time to read it or hold a proper inquiry.","Crossbench members moved amendments to widen family exemptions, protect people in transit, pause visa expiry and pay compensation; none were agreed to.","The Greens said the bill would stop about 7,200 Iranians with valid visas from coming and seeking protection, and called for a larger humanitarian intake.","Greens and some independent senators said the power could be used unfairly against people from particular countries or religions in future."],speeches:73,speakers:29,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-03-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29145/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29209/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29210/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7449:{overview:"This bill makes a range of changes to federal crime laws. It gives police powers at Western Sydney airport, allows electronic warrant applications, extends online surveillance and disruption warrants to 2029, changes how drug quantities are measured, lets a senior officer act for the Director of Public Prosecutions when there is a conflict of interest, and updates extradition arrest powers. The Senate debate focused on the drug changes, the surveillance warrant extension, and the new conflict-of-interest process.",for:["The Government said the changes modernise and streamline crime laws so police and prosecutors can work efficiently while keeping safeguards.","Government members said counting the whole weight of a drug mixture matches state practice and stops gangs using cutting agents to fall under thresholds.","The Government said a single chain-of-custody certificate saves police time, and defendants can still challenge the evidence in court.","The Coalition supported extending the surveillance warrants, saying the security monitor found them effective against serious online crime.","The Government said letting a senior officer act when the DPP has a conflict is more efficient than the DPP taking leave."],against:["The Greens opposed the drug measuring and evidence certificate changes, citing legal aid concerns that they would mostly affect low-level couriers.","The Greens moved to end the surveillance warrants in 2027, saying safeguards recommended by the security monitor should come first. The Coalition and Government opposed this.","The Coalition wanted the removal of the crime intelligence agency's data disruption power considered in a separate bill, and won a review of the new extradition powers.","Coalition senators asked whether the DPP change related to a current war crimes prosecution, and raised concerns about the Attorney-General choosing the substitute. The Government said it did not.","One Nation, backed by the Coalition, moved to restore an earlier legal definition used in war crimes law, saying a 2024 change was unfair to veterans."],speeches:243,speakers:22,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-03-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29145/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29149/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29229/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29230/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29231/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29232/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29178/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29235/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7456:{overview:"This bill doubles the maximum penalty for breaking competition and consumer law, such as misleading customers or price fixing, from $50 million to $100 million per offence. The Government brought it in during a fuel crisis linked to war in the Middle East, saying companies should not use the conflict to overcharge. In the House it was debated together with a separate bill for truck drivers, so much of the debate was about rushed process, fuel prices and supply.",for:["The Government said bigger penalties would let the ACCC come down hard on any company using the war as an excuse for illegal or unfair pricing.","Labor members said the OECD found Australia's penalties were low by world standards, and the change only raises penalties without changing the offences.","Government speakers said the bill is part of a wider fuel response, including releasing fuel reserves and changing fuel standards.","The Coalition supported the bill, and moved in the House to bring it on for an urgent vote.","Independent Zali Steggall supported the bill, saying penalties must be big enough to outweigh any gain from misconduct."],against:["Coalition speakers said the government waited two weeks to bring in the bill, and that higher penalties will not add fuel or lower prices.","Coalition members and independents criticised the government for joining a brand new truckie bill to this debate, leaving no time to read it.","Kate Chaney said doubling penalties was unlikely to change company behaviour, and both major parties were blocking proper scrutiny.","The Greens said the bill does not ban price gouging, and moved an amendment to ban it across the whole economy.","Coalition and One Nation members said cutting fuel excise or GST would give faster relief, and questioned whether the ACCC enforces existing laws."],speeches:78,speakers:54,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-03-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29149/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29150/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29214/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7457:{overview:"This bill makes several tax changes: it removes the $2 minimum for tax-deductible donations, simplifies how trusts report beneficiaries' tax file numbers, and stops gambling and tobacco activities claiming the research and development tax incentive, except research solely to reduce harm. Government amendments added a rise in the Medicare levy low-income thresholds and changes to the pension supplement for people overseas. Debate focused mainly on the gambling and tobacco exclusion and how far it should go.",for:["Government members said removing the $2 limit reflects modern giving, like supermarket round-ups, and supports the goal of doubling philanthropy by 2030.","Labor speakers said taxpayers should not subsidise research that could deepen gambling or tobacco addiction and harm.","The government said trust reporting changes cut duplicate paperwork and help make sure the right amount of tax is paid.","Speakers said raising the Medicare levy thresholds protects over a million low-income earners, and the pension change helps pensioners on short trips overseas.","The Coalition supported the bill's measures in both chambers, calling much of it sensible clean-up work."],against:["Coalition speakers warned that excluding some industries from the R&D incentive could set a precedent for governments picking winners and losers.","Coalition members wanted clarity on what counts as gambling, including whether some children's video games could be caught.","Independents Rebekha Sharkie and Monique Ryan, and the Greens, said the harm minimisation exception should go so gambling and tobacco companies get no R&D support.","Crossbench and Greens speakers called for the government to also act on gambling advertising and the Murphy report's recommendations.","The Greens opposed the pension supplement changes, and One Nation moved to remove the gambling and tobacco exclusion entirely."],speeches:50,speakers:30,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-03-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29149/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29153/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29160/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_28.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29161/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29225/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7458:{overview:"This bill raises penalties for importing, making, selling and possessing illegal tobacco, lets agencies use phone interception and surveillance warrants for serious tobacco offences, and strengthens proceeds of crime and unexplained wealth laws to target criminal profits. All parties who spoke supported it, but the debate focused on whether high tobacco excise has fuelled the illegal market, and whether tougher penalties will work without more enforcement. Speakers cited estimates that illegal products now make up more than half, and possibly up to 80 per cent, of tobacco consumed.",for:["The Government said illegal tobacco is now a serious organised crime problem linked to firebombings and violence, and penalties must outweigh the profits.","Government members said taking criminals' cash, cars and houses through proceeds of crime laws hurts them faster than slow prosecutions.","Government and some crossbench members said cutting excise would make smoking cheaper and undo public health gains, while illegal tobacco would still be cheaper.","Supporters said honest retailers should not have to compete with illegal shops, and pointed to record Border Force seizures and closures in Queensland and South Australia.","Independents including Monique Ryan and Kate Chaney supported the bill but urged caution about tobacco industry influence, and moved a motion calling for a ban on tobacco industry donations."],against:["Coalition speakers said the bill is a minor, late response, and that higher maximum penalties mean little without real enforcement and resources.","Coalition, National and One Nation speakers said high excise rises have driven people to the black market and cut revenue by billions, and called for a review or cuts.","The Coalition moved a motion calling for a full national strategy, including shop closures, lease terminations and stronger coordination with the states.","Allegra Spender and Monique Ryan said enforcement alone will not fix the problem, urging national retail licensing and better state resourcing.","Sophie Scamps moved an amendment to ban imports of tobacco manufacturing equipment without a permit; the Government opposed it on Border Force advice."],speeches:72,speakers:51,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-03-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29150/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29156/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29163/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-22",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29164/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_22.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29235/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7462:{overview:"This bill lets the workplace minister declare an urgent pathway so the Fair Work Commission can make road transport contractual chain orders, such as fuel cost recovery rules, without the usual six-month wait. The Government said it responds to fuel price rises from the Middle East conflict. Most parties supported it, and the debate focused on whether it gives real relief, whether the powers should be permanent, and how fast it was passed.",for:["The Government said truckies and small transport businesses at the end of contract chains cannot pass on surging fuel costs and need faster help.","Government senators said consultation and existing safeguards stay in place, and orders under the urgent pathway last six months.","Government speakers said trucking groups and the Transport Workers' Union had all backed the bill.","The Greens supported the bill as relief for those at the bottom of the supply chain, but said many other workers also need help with fuel costs.","One Nation supported the bill, saying trucking deserves first attention because it affects every Australian quickly."],against:["Coalition senators said the bill gives no immediate relief at the bowser, and called for halving fuel excise and cutting the road user charge instead.","The Coalition said the power is permanent, and moved amendments for a sunset clause, an independent review, required consultation and limiting orders to fuel.","Coalition and One Nation speakers said the bill was rushed through without committee scrutiny and gives the minister broad powers.","One Nation said higher freight charges could push up prices for consumers, and urged use of the Liquid Fuel Emergency Act.","Coalition speakers said only one application had been made under the existing scheme, so the urgency was not justified."],speeches:38,speakers:21,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-03-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29150/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29214/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29215/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7464:{overview:"This bill changes military discipline law to put in place several recommendations of the Royal Commission into Defence and Veteran Suicide. It lets Defence suspend members under civilian police investigation, puts serious service convictions on criminal records, requires reasons for court martial sentences, creates a Defence mental health tribunal, and lets people clear old convictions for consensual homosexual activity. All parties supported it, and the debate focused on protecting victims, mental health, and oversight of new ministerial powers.",for:["Government members said victims of sexual misconduct should not have to keep working with the person being investigated, and the bill closes that gap.","Speakers from all sides said a slow or unfair justice system can harm mental health, and the bill makes processes faster and fairer.","Supporters said recording serious service convictions on civilian criminal records and requiring sentencing reasons will improve accountability.","Labor, the Greens and others welcomed removing the word 'malingering' and allowing old homosexual convictions to be cleared.","The Coalition, Greens, One Nation and independents said they supported the bill as a real step on the royal commission's recommendations."],against:["The Coalition said the minister's new power to guide the Director of Military Prosecutions needed oversight, and the Senate made those guidelines open to parliamentary disallowance.","Coalition senators were concerned about letting civilians act as investigating officers beyond sexual offence cases. The government opposed limiting this.","Coalition member Phillip Thompson raised concerns that military police carrying tasers might mostly affect enlisted soldiers.","Coalition speakers said parts of the bill go beyond the royal commission's advice and leave matters such as medical records to regulations.","Senator Malcolm Roberts read a former member's letter saying military justice can be misused by senior officers, and the bill should only be a beginning."],speeches:30,speakers:19,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29153/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29155/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_13.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29156/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-22",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29221/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_22.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29186/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29243/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7467:{overview:"This bill removes criminal penalties from more than 300 Commonwealth secrecy rules, so most breaches become civil or workplace matters instead. It creates a new, narrower offence for misusing government information for personal gain or to cause harm, and requires the Attorney-General's consent before a journalist can be prosecuted for a secrecy offence. The debate focused on how the new offence is worded, the journalist consent rule and whistleblower protections.",for:["The government said many secrecy offences were outdated or too broad, and criminal law should be kept for serious cases.","Government members said the new offence closes a gap exposed when a former PwC partner allegedly shared confidential government information.","Supporters said making the Attorney-General's consent a law, not just a direction, gives journalists a stronger safeguard.","Crossbench members welcomed the bill as a major step, and the Coalition said it would not oppose it in the House."],against:["The Coalition and crossbench members said the word 'improper' in the new offence is not defined, leaving people unsure what is a crime.","The Coalition said the new offence may not cover leaks made for political reasons with no personal gain, and raised gaps for former officials.","The Coalition said the journalist consent rule was much wider than any review recommended. Crossbench members said it gives a politician power over prosecutions.","Kate Chaney moved amendments for a harm-based test and five-yearly reviews. Allegra Spender moved one to protect whistleblowers. The government opposed both.","Andrew Wilkie moved a motion calling for stronger whistleblower laws and a whistleblower protection authority. The government opposed it."],speeches:23,speakers:14,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29153/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29154/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_12.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29155/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_13.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29156/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7468:{overview:"This bill adds a general ban on unfair trading practices to the Australian Consumer Law, covering conduct that manipulates consumers or unreasonably distorts their choices and causes harm. It also requires mandatory fees to be shown alongside the advertised price, and makes subscriptions clearer and easier to cancel, starting from 1 July 2027. Most speakers supported the rules on hidden fees and subscription traps, and the debate focused on how broad and clear the general ban is, its cost for small business, and whether it should cover financial services.",for:["The Government said some harmful practices, like fake countdown timers or hard-to-find cancel buttons, fall short of being misleading or unconscionable, leaving a gap in the law.","Government members said unwanted subscriptions cost Australians an estimated $971 million a year, and cancelling should be as easy as signing up.","Supporters said showing all mandatory fees upfront helps honest businesses that already show the full price, and does not ban fees themselves.","The Government said the ACCC and 17 consumer groups backed the bill, and the ACCC will provide guidance with an education-first approach.","The Greens, One Nation and independent Monique Ryan supported the bill as better than the current law."],against:["Coalition speakers said the general ban is vague and could lead to costly court cases, with Treasury estimating over $100 million a year in costs for small businesses.","The Coalition moved amendments to add 'unfairly' before 'manipulate' and 'material' before 'detriment', and to delay the rules for small businesses until 2030; the Government opposed them.","The Greens moved amendments to cover financial services and name 'lead generation' as unfair, citing regulators' support; the Government said financial services may come later.","Monique Ryan said the general ban may be too narrow to enforce, no single total price is required, and bigger issues like market concentration are left out.","One Nation said the broad ban hands too much power to the regulator to decide what is unfair."],speeches:90,speakers:35,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29153/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29154/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_12.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29155/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_13.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29156/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29227/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29228/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7469:{overview:"This bill sets up a new consumer protection system for air travel, with an independent Aviation Consumer Ombudsperson for complaints, a charter of minimum standards for airlines and airports, and a regulator to enforce it. It also moves the Aircraft Noise Ombudsperson into the transport department, away from Airservices Australia. The debate focused on whether passengers should get automatic compensation for delays, how regional airlines and airports would be treated, and how much detail was left to regulations.",for:["The government said the industry-run complaints body had failed and passengers now get a free, independent ombudsperson that can make binding decisions.","Government members said passengers will get cash refunds instead of forced credits and help with meals, transport and hotels when disruptions are the airline's fault.","The government said automatic compensation can push up fares and threaten thin regional routes without improving on-time performance.","Members from areas near airports welcomed a noise ombudsperson separate from Airservices Australia."],against:["The Coalition said the bill adds bureaucracy but no direct compensation, and should cover Airservices Australia and protect unused COVID flight credits.","Coalition and crossbench members said the regional airport exemption should be written into the law, not left to the minister.","Crossbench MPs and the Greens said the charter was not in the bill and called for fixed compensation like in Europe and Canada.","The Greens said the noise ombudsperson is still not truly independent and called for action on Brisbane aircraft noise."],speeches:51,speakers:37,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29153/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29167/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29168/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29226/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7470:{overview:"This bill lets the minister set a yearly levy on the aviation industry to pay the costs of a new Aviation Consumer Protection Authority. It is part of a package of bills on aviation consumer protection. The transcript only contains the minister's opening speech and the final vote, with no debate.",for:["The Government said the levy would recover the cost of running the new consumer protections from the aviation industry.","The Government said the minister must consider whether each company pays a fair share of the total levy."],against:[],speeches:2,speakers:1,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29153/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29168/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7471:{overview:"This bill sets out how levies will be collected from regulated businesses to cover the running costs of the new Aviation Consumer Protection Authority. The authority is set up by a separate bill as part of the transport department. The transcript only includes the minister's introduction and the final vote, with no recorded debate.",for:["The Government said the bill lets the costs of the new aviation consumer protection regulator be recovered from the businesses it regulates.","The Government said it sets clear steps for collecting and managing the charges, in line with the separate levy bill."],against:[],speeches:2,speakers:1,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29153/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29168/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7472:{overview:"This bill gives Export Finance Australia new powers to buy, sell, stockpile and underwrite fuel, critical minerals and other strategic materials when supply chains are disrupted. It was rushed through during a fuel crisis caused by the war in the Middle East, and also sets up the $1.2 billion Critical Minerals Strategic Reserve. The bill passed with wide support, and debate focused on the government's handling of the fuel crisis, domestic oil and gas, and how broad the new powers are.",for:["The government said fuel cargoes are becoming too risky for companies to buy, so underwriting them helps keep fuel coming to Australia, especially to regional areas.","Labor members said the critical minerals reserve will steady prices, support jobs and work with partners like the United States and Japan.","The Coalition, One Nation and most crossbench members supported the bill as a sensible emergency step to secure fuel and fertiliser.","Supporters said it builds on other actions, including releasing fuel stocks and halving fuel excise for three months."],against:["The Coalition said the government was slow to act, and the bill will not refill empty service stations or lower prices quickly.","Coalition speakers said it is inconsistent to fund imported fuel while limiting support for local oil and gas. Their amendments to remove those limits failed.","Coalition members said small fuel distributors and service stations were left carrying price risk while large importers were supported.","Independents and the Greens said the powers are too broad and permanent. The Greens moved to end them after six months and bar use for weapons.","Crossbench members said the first review in 2029 is too late, and taxpayers should share any profits as well as losses."],speeches:83,speakers:41,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-03-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29151/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29216/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_31.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7475:{overview:"This bill supports a new aviation consumer protection law. The minister said it makes clear that an airline breaking the consumer charter will not lose its licence under the Air Navigation Act, and it moves the Aircraft Noise Ombudsperson to a new independent, legislated role. The transcript only covers the minister's speech and the final vote.",for:["The minister said the bill gives clarity about the consequences for airlines that breach the charter.","The minister said it allows a smooth move to an independent, statutory Aircraft Noise Ombudsperson."],against:[],speeches:2,speakers:1,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29153/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29168/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7481:{overview:"This bill makes about 20 small changes across 25 laws to cut red tape. It changes trademark and plant breeder rules, speeds some anti-dumping refunds, eases gender equality reporting, and takes 'tell us once' steps so people don't have to give government the same information twice. Debate focused on whether it goes far enough, a change to Human Rights Commission complaint notices, and a late change to the Compensation Scheme of Last Resort.",for:["The government said the changes reduce compliance costs and form part of a plan to cut regulation costs by $10.2 billion a year.","Government members said 'tell us once' changes help pensioners overseas and people cancelling Centrelink nominee arrangements, including in family violence cases.","The government said ending compulsory Human Rights Commission notices spares uninvolved people stress, and the commission president can still notify people.","The government said shortening the parliamentary review period for a compensation levy gets money to victims of financial misconduct faster. The Greens supported this with a time limit."],against:["The Coalition said removing compulsory notices means people named in discrimination complaints may not learn of allegations, and moved to remove that part.","The Coalition, Pocock and Spender said the bill is only a small step. Spender and Pocock moved motions calling for red tape reduction targets.","Helen Haines and the Coalition objected to dropping advertising spending reports from annual reports. The government removed that part.","One Nation raised privacy concerns about data sharing between agencies and sought Senate inquiries. The Greens moved to ask arrivals about foreign military service."],speeches:109,speakers:30,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-05-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29155/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_13.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29166/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29170/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29228/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-07",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29236/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_07.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29239/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29183/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29240/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7482:{overview:"This bill provides $345.4 million for the parliament's own departments in 2026-27, including about $274 million for the Department of Parliamentary Services, which runs Parliament House. It was debated together with the main budget bills, so speakers talked about the whole 2026-27 budget rather than this bill itself. Most of the debate was about the budget's changes to negative gearing, capital gains tax and family trusts, plus tax cuts, health and housing.",for:["Government members said limiting negative gearing to new builds and changing capital gains tax will help about 75,000 more people buy their first home.","Labor speakers said the budget gives workers more tax cuts, including a $250 offset and a $1,000 instant deduction, worth up to about $2,800 a year.","Government members pointed to permanent funding for Medicare urgent care clinics, cheaper medicines and $25 billion more for public hospitals.","Supporters said fuel reserves, a domestic gas reserve and $2 billion for housing infrastructure make Australia more resilient to global shocks."],against:["Coalition speakers said the Prime Minister broke repeated promises not to change negative gearing or capital gains tax and has no mandate for it.","Coalition and crossbench members said the capital gains, trust and negative gearing changes hurt small businesses, farmers, retirees and young people saving through shares.","Opponents criticised a $5,000 yearly cap on veterans' allied health, lower private health rebates for over-65s, and cuts to Inland Rail and regional programs.","The Greens and some independents moved amendments calling for a 25 per cent tax on gas exports; the Greens also sought to reverse NDIS cuts.","Independents Rebekha Sharkie and Zali Steggall asked the government to take the tax changes to an election or release more modelling first."],speeches:87,speakers:63,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-05-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29154/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_12.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29157/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29158/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29159/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_27.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29160/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_28.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29163/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-22",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29221/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_22.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29225/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7483:{overview:"This bill approves the government's main day-to-day spending for 2026-27 and carries the federal budget. The Treasurer said the budget responds to an oil shock from war in the Middle East with a $14.8 billion fuel security plan, new tax relief for workers, savings mainly from the NDIS, and changes to negative gearing and capital gains tax. The debate was wide-ranging, with most attention on those tax changes, housing, spending and inflation, the NDIS, defence and fuel.",for:["The Government said the budget helps Australians through a global oil shock with fuel reserves, more fertiliser, and a new $250 tax offset for 13.3 million workers.","Labor members said limiting negative gearing to new builds and changing capital gains tax would give first home buyers a fairer chance against investors.","Government speakers pointed to $25 billion more for public hospitals, permanent urgent care clinics, cheaper medicines and more bulk-billing.","The Government said record savings and low spending growth strengthen the budget, and NDIS changes would keep the scheme sustainable.","Labor members said defence funding rises by $53 billion over a decade, alongside more money for aged care, schools and employment services."],against:["The Coalition said the capital gains, negative gearing and trust changes break election promises and will hurt investment, small business and farm succession.","Coalition speakers cited budget papers saying the tax changes mean 35,000 fewer homes, and said high spending is keeping inflation and interest rates up.","Coalition members raised concerns about NDIS eligibility cuts, a cap on veterans' allied health, cuts to drought and research funding, and lower private health rebates for over-65s.","Independents called for tighter spending, more transparency on off-budget items, and more funding for prevention, nature and foreign aid.","The Greens criticised the budget for not taxing gas exports and moved an amendment for a 25 per cent tax on gas export revenue."],speeches:143,speakers:87,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-05-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29154/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_12.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29156/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29161/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29162/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29163/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7484:{overview:"This is one of the 2026-27 budget bills. It seeks $33.7 billion for spending that is not part of the ordinary annual services of government, including money for Defence, Finance, Treasury housing loans and infrastructure projects, plus a $3.6 billion reserve for urgent and unforeseen costs. The transcript records only the minister's introduction and the bill passing the House without further debate.",for:["The minister said the budget aims to ease pressure on ordinary Australians through tax changes, including a new $250 tax offset for workers.","The minister said changes to trusts, capital gains tax and negative gearing would make the system fairer and help more people buy a home.","The minister said transparency safeguards apply to the urgent spending reserve, including consulting the shadow finance minister on allocations over $1 billion."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:3,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-05-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29154/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_12.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29163/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7487:{overview:"This bill makes major changes to the NDIS, including moving eligibility from diagnosis to a functional capacity assessment, requiring appropriate treatment before a disability counts as permanent, limiting unscheduled plan reviews, letting the minister cut funding for groups of supports such as community participation, giving the minister control of prices, and expanding fraud powers and automated decisions. The debate focused on cost growth, fraud, the community participation cuts, the undefined assessment and whether other supports would be ready. The bill passed with many amendments.",for:["The Government said the scheme is growing too fast and could cost $100 billion a year next decade, risking public support, so reform is needed to secure it.","Government members said the changes return the NDIS to its original purpose of supporting people with permanent and significant disability, following the independent NDIS review.","The Government said new registration rules, investigation powers, civil penalties and a 90-day claim limit will help tackle fraud that harms participants.","Government speakers said community participation spending tripled to $12 billion in five years, and a $200 million fund and foundational supports like Thriving Kids will help.","The Coalition supported the bill, saying the scheme must be sustainable, and backed stronger registration and integrity measures, while seeking changes to protect daily living supports."],against:["The Greens opposed the bill, saying it would push about 241,000 people off the scheme and that revenue could be raised elsewhere, such as a gas export tax.","Crossbench and Coalition speakers said eligibility rules and the assessment tool had not been designed, leaving participants uncertain, and that consultation was rushed.","Many speakers said the 'all appropriate treatment' test could exclude people who cannot afford or reach treatment, especially in regional areas.","Critics warned the 50 per cent cut to community participation funding could isolate people and increase risks of abuse, and that replacement services were not ready.","Crossbench members and the Greens raised concerns about broad ministerial powers, minister-set pricing and automated decisions, citing robodebt. Coalition speakers said fraud measures did not go far enough."],speeches:261,speakers:89,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-05-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29156/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29158/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29159/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_27.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29160/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_28.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-22",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29221/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_22.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29170/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29171/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29230/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29232/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-18",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29233/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_18.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7490:{overview:"This bill writes tariff changes already in effect into the Customs Tariff Act. It sets about 500 low-value 'nuisance' tariffs to free, extends duty-free entry for most goods from Ukraine, and extends an extra 35 per cent duty on goods from Russia and Belarus. Both major parties supported it, with the Coalition asking for more detail on how the tariffs were chosen.",for:["The government said removing nuisance tariffs cuts red tape and saves businesses about $157 million a year in compliance costs.","Government and Coalition speakers said most of these goods already came in duty-free through concessions or free trade deals.","Both sides supported extending duty-free Ukrainian goods and the extra duty on Russia and Belarus as a show of support for Ukraine.","A Labor member said simpler tariffs help small businesses that have no staff to handle paperwork."],against:["Coalition speakers said the government should explain how the tariffs were chosen and what modelling it did on local manufacturing.","The Coalition said cost-of-living benefits may be overstated, as savings may not reach shoppers.","A Nationals member called for a Senate inquiry to examine the detail and questioned why some unions were consulted.","One Nation moved a Senate amendment calling for protection of the local timber industry from dumped imports and for country of origin labels."],speeches:11,speakers:8,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-05-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29159/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_27.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29166/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29224/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29235/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7491:{overview:"This bill gives Medicare's GP incentive payment programs, such as the Bulk Billing Practice Incentive Program, a clear legal framework for how they are run, checked and reviewed, and renames the Health Insurance Act the Medicare Act. It does not change who is eligible or how much is paid. The Coalition did not oppose it, but much of the debate was about bulk-billing rates and out-of-pocket costs, and the Senate added a Coalition safeguard on computer-made decisions, which the House accepted.",for:["The Government said these programs, worth over $1.4 billion a year, had no proper legal basis, and the bill gives providers and government certainty.","Government members said the bill protects public money with consistent compliance and debt recovery rules, plus review rights through the Administrative Review Tribunal.","Labor speakers said bulk-billing incentives are working, with the national rate rising to about 82 per cent and more practices fully bulk-billing.","Government members said renaming the law the Medicare Act better reflects what it covers and how Australians know it.","Coalition speakers said they supported the incentive programs, having set up several in government, and backed proper administration."],against:["Coalition speakers said GP out-of-pocket costs hit a record of about $50 a visit and bulk-billing remains below the 88 per cent reached under the Coalition.","The Coalition moved amendments saying the Government broke its promise that a Medicare card is all you need to see a GP.","Nationals members said the bill is paperwork, not patient care, and does nothing to fix doctor shortages or maternity services in regional areas.","Coalition members said Labor MPs pressured family GP clinics to bulk-bill, and the bill needed Senate committee scrutiny.","The Coalition sought an expert advisory group, rules for discretionary powers and limits on automated decisions; the automation safeguard was passed."],speeches:45,speakers:27,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-05-27",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29159/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_27.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29168/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29170/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29171/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29228/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-16",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29242/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_16.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-10-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29188/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_10_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7492:{overview:"This bill sets a 30 per cent minimum tax on capital gains from 1 July 2027. It was debated together with a companion bill that swaps the 50 per cent capital gains discount for inflation indexing, limits negative gearing to new homes, and adds a $250 worker tax offset and a $1,000 standard deduction. Most of the debate was about the capital gains and negative gearing changes, how they affect housing, small business, startups and investors, and whether the bills were being rushed.",for:["Government members said taxing only gains above inflation is fairer than the 50 per cent discount, which they said has pushed investment towards existing homes since 1999.","Supporters said limiting negative gearing to new builds steers investment into new housing and will help about 75,000 more people buy a home. Existing investments are grandfathered.","Government members said the $250 offset and $1,000 deduction cut tax for over 13 million workers and make tax time simpler. Coalition members said they supported these two parts.","The government said the minimum tax stops people delaying gains until low-income years. Pension and JobSeeker recipients are exempt, and small business concessions were widened in Senate amendments.","Several independents and the Greens backed the housing tax changes in principle, saying property tax breaks have favoured investors over first home buyers."],against:["Coalition members opposed the capital gains and negative gearing changes. They said these broke an election promise, raise $77 billion in tax and mean 35,000 fewer homes.","Coalition and Nationals speakers said the changes would hurt small businesses, family farms, trusts and self-funded retirees paying 30 per cent on modest gains. They promised repeal.","Independents said extending the changes beyond property would hurt startups and young share investors. Kate Chaney moved to limit them to property; others wanted a longer inquiry.","The Greens said the bill did not go far enough. They argued grandfathering for investors with many properties was too generous and left large tax breaks in place.","Crossbench senators said the process was rushed and gave the Treasurer too much power. David Pocock withdrew amendments on death and divorce after government assurances."],speeches:420,speakers:131,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-05-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29160/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_28.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29161/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29162/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29163/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-22",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29221/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_22.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29222/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_23.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29223/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29224/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7493:{overview:"This bill gives workers a yearly tax offset of up to $250 and a $1,000 instant deduction for work expenses. It also replaces the 50 per cent capital gains tax discount with inflation indexing and a 30 per cent minimum tax, and limits negative gearing on newly bought existing homes to new builds. The debate focused on housing, fairness between wages and investment income, and how the bill was put together.",for:["The government said the bill cuts tax for over 13 million workers and puts more money in their pockets.","Government members said limiting negative gearing and changing capital gains tax would help about 75,000 more people buy their first home over ten years.","The Treasurer said indexing gains to inflation treats all investments the same and means some people will pay less tax.","The government said small business tax concessions stay in place and current property owners keep their negative gearing until they sell.","Independent members Zali Steggall and Allegra Spender said they backed the aim of reforming housing tax settings."],against:["Coalition speakers said the government had no election mandate and had ruled out these changes before the election.","The Coalition argued the negative gearing changes would reduce housing supply and raise rents, citing the budget papers and industry modelling.","The Coalition supported the tax cuts but moved to remove the capital gains and negative gearing parts, and called for tax brackets to be indexed to inflation.","Coalition speakers said the bill gave the Treasurer too much power to decide details without Parliament.","Independent members moved amendments to limit the capital gains changes to property, protect active businesses and small businesses, and said the bill was rushed."],speeches:31,speakers:12,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-05-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29160/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_28.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29161/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29163/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29167/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29168/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7494:{overview:"This bill gives the Fair Work Commission new tools to handle a sharp rise in cases, including skipping some preliminary hearings, deciding matters on the papers with consent, and dismissing vexatious claims. It also creates a separate income threshold so more truck drivers can challenge unfair contracts, and lets the Commonwealth favour businesses with enterprise agreements when giving out contracts and grants. Most of the debate was about that last change, with the Senate adding a Greens amendment so workers cannot lose conditions in arbitrated agreements.",for:["Government members said the commission's workload has jumped, partly due to AI-written claims, and the changes help it resolve genuine disputes faster without cutting workers' rights.","Labor speakers said truck drivers' high running costs make the current income test unfair, so a separate threshold lets more access protections.","The government said public money should be able to back employers who bargain in good faith, and the change creates an option, not an obligation.","The Greens supported the procurement change and added protections so workers cannot go backwards when bargaining breaks down.","The Coalition and independents supported the Fair Work Commission changes as practical and sensible."],against:["The Coalition said the procurement change lets government favour businesses with union agreements, hurting small businesses that use awards, and opposed the bill unless it was split.","Coalition speakers pointed to the Watson report on CFMEU corruption and Queensland's past policy, saying the change risks higher costs and corruption.","Independents Kate Chaney, Allegra Spender and Helen Haines said contracts should be awarded on merit, and moved to remove the procurement part.","Critics said key details are left to the minister and an unreleased jobs code, and that business groups opposed the change.","The Coalition said the truck driver income threshold needed more scrutiny through a Senate inquiry."],speeches:79,speakers:41,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-06-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29162/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29166/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29225/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29169/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7497:{overview:"This bill would remove the higher private health insurance rebate for people aged 65 and over from April 2027, so the rebate depends on income only, not age. The Government said the roughly $3 billion saved would go to aged care. The debate focused on fairness between generations, the cost to older people and pensioners, and whether people dropping or downgrading cover would add pressure on public hospitals.",for:["Government members said two households on the same income should get the same rebate, whatever their age.","The Government said the roughly $3 billion saved would fund more aged-care beds, Support at Home places and dementia care.","Government speakers said the rebate stays means tested at up to 24 per cent, with an average rise of about $250 a year.","The Government cited modelling that about 44,000 fewer older people would be insured, a small change with marginal hospital impact.","Independent Allegra Spender supported the bill as better means testing, citing academic modelling that hospital effects would be manageable."],against:["Coalition members said older people kept cover for decades as governments urged, and now face higher costs on fixed incomes, with some couples paying up to $1,614 more.","Opponents argued people dropping or downgrading cover would shift costs to state public hospitals, citing industry modelling and concerns from state governments.","Coalition and crossbench speakers said regional private hospitals and private maternity services could be put at risk.","Independents including Monique Ryan and Kate Chaney said about half the savings come from pensioners, moved to exempt them, and said downgrades were not modelled.","Coalition speakers said the change was not taken to the election and questioned whether the savings would actually reach aged care."],speeches:134,speakers:61,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-06-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29167/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29182/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29183/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-15",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29184/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_15.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-16",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29185/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_16.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29243/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7499:{overview:"This bill changes how major sporting events get protection for their logos and images against fake merchandise and 'ambush marketing'. Instead of parliament passing a new law for each event, the minister would list events in rules that parliament can still disallow. The debate focused less on the protections themselves, which all sides supported, and more on whether this moves too much power from parliament to the minister.",for:["Government members said the change removes the need to pass a new law each time Australia hosts a major event, such as Brisbane 2032.","Supporters said protecting sponsors from ambush marketing keeps private investment in events and reduces the cost to governments.","Government members said clear criteria, such as international significance, and parliament's power to disallow the rules provide safeguards.","Supporters said fans should know they are buying genuine merchandise, and exemptions for reporting and commentary remain.","One Nation supported the bill, saying it cuts red tape and cost while the Senate can still vote down an instrument."],against:["Coalition speakers said the protections already exist and the bill only shifts the decision from parliament to the minister.","The Coalition said parliament would only see decisions after they become law, which reduces transparency and scrutiny.","Coalition members said no stakeholders asked for the change, there was no urgency, and events are announced years ahead.","In the Senate, the Coalition said it would oppose the bill as a solution without a clear problem."],speeches:25,speakers:18,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-06-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29167/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29169/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29170/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29227/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-07",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29236/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_07.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29240/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7500:{overview:"This bill makes small technical fixes to 42 Commonwealth laws, such as correcting errors, updating names and references, and removing spent parts. It also repeals 106 old laws that no longer do anything. In the Senate, the debate turned mostly to amendments added to the bill to stop a planned $5,000 yearly limit on veterans' allied health care.",for:["Government members said bills like this have been passed since 1934 and keep the law accurate, clear and easier for everyone to read and use.","Labor's Tom French said small drafting errors, like a wrong cross-reference or misplaced punctuation, can cause real legal problems and should be fixed.","Coalition members said they support keeping the statute book up to date and would take the government at its word that the changes are technical.","The government said the changes have no financial impact and do not change anyone's rights except in minor ways."],against:["The Coalition said parliament should check, not assume, that every change is technical, and wanted a Senate committee to look at changes touching veterans, criminal and electoral law.","The Coalition, Greens, One Nation and crossbench senators backed amendments to ban a $5,000 cost limit or review trigger on veterans' allied health care.","The government opposed those amendments, saying they could have unintended effects and would cut across consultation with veterans that is still under way.","One Nation moved an amendment to restore an older war-crimes definition, saying a 2024 change should not apply back to past military service."],speeches:24,speakers:19,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-06-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29167/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29170/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29171/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29228/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29239/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29183/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7501:{overview:"This bill changes how university places are funded. The minister sets a total pool of Commonwealth supported places, the Australian Tertiary Education Commission shares them out, and extra 'needs based' funding follows students from poor backgrounds, First Nations students and students at regional campuses. It also sets up a system for allocating international student places, and the debate focused on ministerial power, student choice and the over-enrolment limits, with the bill passing after many Senate amendments.",for:["The government said the bill funds about 230,000 extra students over a decade and effectively uncaps places for poorer and regional students who get the marks.","Government members said needs based funding, more than double past support per student, will pay for tutoring, mentoring and grants that help students finish.","Regional MPs and universities said managed growth stops big city universities over-enrolling at the expense of regional ones.","The Coalition backed the final bill after winning amendments to keep regional funding for genuinely regional campuses and keep powers over international student numbers."],against:["Coalition and crossbench members said the bill gave the minister too much power, including to set conditions on individual universities without disallowance or review.","Speakers warned hard caps on over-enrolment could reduce student choice and lead universities to make fewer offers.","Crossbench MPs and the Greens said it leaves Job-ready Graduates fees in place and does little about student poverty or debt.","The Greens raised concerns about cutting international student numbers, while One Nation opposed central planning and funding based on identity groups.","Some speakers said the low-income measure is outdated and misses disadvantaged students in places like the ACT."],speeches:104,speakers:68,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-06-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29167/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29171/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29174/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29175/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29178/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-07",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29236/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_07.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-15",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29241/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_15.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-16",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29242/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_16.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29243/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-10-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29187/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_10_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-10-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29245/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_10_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7503:{overview:"This bill extends cuts to fuel excise for another month, from 1 July to early August 2026, after the conflict in the Middle East pushed up fuel prices. The cut falls from about 32c to 16c per litre, tapering support before normal settings return. Debate covered cost of living, whether the relief should be paid for with savings, and fuel tax credits for large mining companies.",for:["The government said people are still under cost-of-living pressure, and the extension saves about $11 a tank while easing back to normal settings.","Labor speakers said tapering the cut avoids a sudden price jump for families, truckies and small businesses as fuel markets recover.","Coalition speakers supported the bill, saying the excise cut was first their idea and that people in regional areas need affordable fuel.","Crossbench members, including Monique Ryan and Dai Le, supported the taper because diesel prices remained high and many people have no option but to drive."],against:["Coalition speakers said the government had not offered savings to offset the roughly $400 million cost, which they argued adds to inflation.","Coalition and Nationals members said the bill was a short-term fix and Australia needs a long-term fuel security plan, including local refining.","Independent Nicolette Boele moved an amendment calling for a $50 million yearly cap per company on fuel tax credits; the Greens moved a similar Senate amendment.","Crossbench members asked for data showing how much of the excise cut was passed on to drivers at the bowser.","In the Senate, One Nation moved an amendment calling on the government to cancel the excise increase and suspend indexation."],speeches:36,speakers:18,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-06-22",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29164/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_22.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29165/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_23.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29225/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7507:{overview:"This bill updates the Foreign Arrangements Scheme, which oversees deals that states, territories, councils and public universities make with foreign bodies. It follows an independent review and broadens the test to Australia's national interest, lets conditions be put on deals, and cuts paperwork for low-risk deals. The debate focused on whether research collaborations made directly by university researchers should also be covered.",for:["The Government said the review found the scheme works but should focus on high-risk deals and cut red tape for routine ones like sister city links.","Government members said allowing conditions, rather than just yes or no, is a more proportionate way to manage risk.","Supporters said the bill improves information sharing across government and covers overseas campuses of Australian universities for the first time.","The Coalition supported the bill, saying it created the scheme in 2020 after Victoria's Belt and Road agreement with China."],against:["The Coalition said the bill left a gap because researchers could work with foreign researchers without the university having to notify the Commonwealth.","Coalition speakers pointed to media reports of Australian researchers working with Iranian researchers on drone technology, and said ministers' letters were not enough.","The Government rejected the Coalition amendment, saying espionage and foreign interference laws already cover researchers, then moved its own amendment requiring universities to have policies to manage such projects.","The Coalition welcomed the Government amendment as a step forward but said it still did not create a legal duty to notify."],speeches:25,speakers:14,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29170/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29178/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29235/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7512:{overview:"This bill strengthens enforcement of the law banning under-16s from holding social media accounts. It doubles the maximum penalty for platforms that break the rules and lets the eSafety Commissioner demand information from anyone holding relevant evidence; Senate amendments also added powers to question people under oath. The debate focused on whether the ban is working, how far the new powers should reach, and calls for a wider digital duty of care.",for:["The Government said platforms are doing the bare minimum, such as letting children retry age checks, and stronger penalties and powers are needed to hold them to account.","Labor members said the Commissioner needs evidence from third parties, like age-check providers, to test what platforms claim they are doing.","Government speakers pointed to over five million underage accounts removed and survey results showing better sleep and less online bullying.","Several independents, including Kate Chaney and Sophie Scamps, supported the bill as a sensible step, while urging a broader digital duty of care.","The Greens backed the added questioning powers, saying the Commissioner had asked for them, though they called the original ban a failure."],against:["Coalition speakers said the bill admits the ban was badly designed, citing studies finding most under-16s still use social media.","The Coalition opposed the Senate changes, saying powers to demand documents and question people under oath went too far and could reach parents and children.","One Nation opposed the bill, saying children's online use is a matter for parents and the new powers threaten free speech and rights.","Independents and Greens said even doubled fines are small for large tech companies; the Greens proposed penalties of 10 per cent of global turnover.","Several members said 'reasonable steps' is still undefined and that a ban alone cannot fix unsafe platform design or harmful algorithms."],speeches:148,speakers:54,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-06-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29168/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_29.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-06-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29169/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_06_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29170/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29237/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_08.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29238/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_09.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29182/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29239/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7513:{overview:"This bill supports the main Cash Distribution Framework Bill, which was debated alongside it. Together they let the Reserve Bank name key cash-transport companies, give the ACCC oversight of their prices and service levels, and set up emergency powers and up to $400 million in last-resort funding to keep cash moving. This bill also gives the ACCC temporary powers to give directions during the changeover, and the debate focused on access to cash and whether the government acted quickly enough.",for:["Government members said about one in ten Australians still use cash for most purchases, especially older, lower-income and regional people.","Supporters said one company now moves most of Australia's cash, so a regulator is needed to keep prices fair and service reliable.","Speakers from all sides said cash keeps working during power cuts, network outages and natural disasters.","Government members said amendments make sure the new rules do not override Fair Work Commission safety and pay orders for transport workers.","Coalition and One Nation speakers said they would not oppose the bills, calling cash critical national infrastructure."],against:["The Coalition moved an amendment saying the government acted too late, since risks were clear after a 2023 merger and a near-crisis at Easter 2024.","Coalition speakers said the bills get cash delivered but not accepted, and the cash mandate is too narrow, for example not covering pharmacies.","The Coalition and others said there is no plan for when the pause on regional bank branch closures ends in July 2027.","Independent Andrew Gee moved an amendment calling for businesses to accept cash for purchases up to $10,000.","One Nation's Malcolm Roberts warned the $400 million crisis funding could be triggered too easily, and independent Dai Le said compliance costs could reach consumers."],speeches:46,speakers:34,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29171/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29172/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29173/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29232/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29235/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7514:{overview:"This bill gives the Tax Practitioners Board stronger penalties and powers to deal with tax adviser misconduct, in response to the PwC tax leaks matter. It also makes foreign investors pay capital gains tax on more assets tied to Australian land, with a 50 per cent discount for renewable energy assets, plus changes to merger rules and charity listings. Most debate focused on the renewable energy discount, which the government extended from 2030 to 2040.",for:["The government said stronger, graduated penalties will deter misconduct and protect honest tax agents and their clients.","Government members said foreign investors should not pay less tax than Australians on gains from Australian land and resources.","Crossbench MPs and the Greens said the renewable discount must run to 2040 to match long investment timelines and keep clean energy investment flowing.","Speakers welcomed the merger rule fixes as cutting needless red tape, including for venture capital."],against:["The Coalition said the tax change could deter foreign investment in big energy and industry projects, and sought a Senate inquiry, which did not happen.","Coalition and One Nation senators asked why only renewable energy investors get a discount, and called the 2040 extension a deal with the Greens.","One Nation moved to remove the renewable discount entirely, saying it costs taxpayers and favours foreign companies.","Some crossbench MPs said the change was not just a clarification and existing investors deserved fairer transition rules.","Speakers criticised bundling eight unrelated schedules into one bill."],speeches:95,speakers:32,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29171/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29178/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-07",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29236/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_07.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29239/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7517:{overview:"This bill creates a permanent, independent Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander Aged Care Commissioner, as the aged care royal commission recommended. It also makes technical fixes to the new Aged Care Act, and a later government amendment added a new review pathway for decisions made by the aged care assessment tool. All sides backed the commissioner, but much of the debate focused on the assessment tool and wider problems in aged care.",for:["The government said older First Nations people use aged care less despite greater need, and a commissioner will give them a stronger voice.","Government members said the role was shaped by wide consultation by the interim commissioner with over 1,000 older First Nations people.","The Coalition supported the commissioner and called the technical fixes sensible.","The government said its amendment lets assessors ask for a human review when the tool cannot capture a person's needs."],against:["Coalition speakers said needing fixes within a year showed the new Aged Care Act was rushed, and the bill does nothing for waiting lists or new beds.","Crossbench members and the Greens said the assessment tool is badly flawed and clinicians need a full power to override it.","Some crossbench members said the review rules are left to regulations and the seven-day window is too short.","The Greens moved to split the assessment tool changes into a separate bill, which the government opposed.","David Farley called for a similar commissioner for culturally and linguistically diverse older Australians."],speeches:28,speakers:21,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29170/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29180/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_08.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29181/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_09.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29186/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-10-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29245/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_10_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7520:{overview:"This bill limits gambling ads on TV, radio and online, bans them from sports jerseys and stadiums, and stops celebrities and influencers promoting betting. It also targets illegal offshore gambling sites, strengthens the BetStop self-exclusion register and bans online keno and some lottery products. The debate focused on whether it went far enough compared with the 2023 Murphy report, especially on inducements and the online opt-out model, and the Government and Coalition agreed amendments before it passed.",for:["Government members said it is the biggest gambling reform yet, banning ads during daytime live sport, capping TV gambling ads at three an hour and clearing jerseys and stadiums.","Supporters argued children should grow up loving sport without seeing betting as part of it, and that banning celebrity and influencer promotion protects young people.","Government speakers said online platforms must check users are logged-in adults who can opt out, or else show no gambling ads to anyone.","Members backed banning online keno and closing foreign and fake lottery loopholes, saying this protects consumers, newsagents and charities.","Coalition speakers said the amendments they negotiated, covering inducements to at-risk customers, children's programs, online live sport, a single opt-out register and a review, made the bill much stronger."],against:["Crossbench members and the Greens said the bill ignores most Murphy report recommendations, including a full phased ad ban, a ban on inducements and a national regulator.","Critics argued partial bans just move ads to other times, pointing to three ads an hour during the day and no limit after 8.30 pm.","Many members said opt-out systems are rarely used, citing very low take-up on SBS, and called for people to opt in to gambling ads instead.","Crossbench and Greens speakers linked the bill's limits to gambling industry donations and lobbying, while Government members rejected claims about their motives.","Some Coalition members opposed banning foreign matched lotteries, saying it would cost about 50 jobs and that the evidence of harm had not been shown."],speeches:395,speakers:83,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29171/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29173/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29174/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-18",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29176/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_18.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29234/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7521:{overview:"This bill expands the cost-recovery levy for BetStop, the national register people can use to exclude themselves from online gambling. It lets the levy cover more of the work done by ACMA and the department, including promotion and awareness campaigns. The transcript covers its introduction and passage, with no debate.",for:["The minister said a review found only one in four people know BetStop exists, so more promotion is needed.","The minister said gambling companies, not taxpayers, should pay for this work."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29171/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-18",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29176/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_18.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7522:{overview:"This bill sets up rules to keep cash moving around Australia as fewer people use it. It lets the Reserve Bank pick key cash transport and processing businesses for oversight, gives the ACCC power over their prices and service standards, and gives the Reserve Bank crisis powers backed by up to $400 million. The transcript mostly covers the minister's speech and procedural votes, including government amendments about how the law fits with Fair Work orders for cash-in-transit workers.",for:["The government said Australians who want or need cash, especially in regional areas, older people and small businesses, should not be pushed out of the economy.","It said falling cash use is straining the business of moving cash, so oversight is needed to keep prices fair and services reliable.","It said crisis powers and last-resort funding would help stop disruptions, with unrecovered costs charged to major users of cash services.","Government amendments were moved so that Fair Work orders covering cash-in-transit work can take priority over competing obligations where needed."],against:["Amendments moved by the members for Calare and Page were put to a vote; the Calare amendment was defeated, but the transcript does not record what either amendment said."],speeches:7,speakers:3,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29171/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29173/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29178/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7524:{overview:"This bill sets the charge behind the News Bargaining Incentive: a levy on the Australian digital advertising revenue of large search and social media platforms, which they can offset by doing deals with Australian news businesses. A government amendment raised the rate from 2.5 to 2.75 per cent. The debate, held together with related bills, focused on whether the scheme would help small, local and independent publishers and whether AI companies should be covered.",for:["The government said platforms profit from Australian news and should help pay for it, either through commercial deals or by paying the charge.","Government members said the charge fixes a gap in the old bargaining code, because platforms can no longer avoid it by dropping news.","Supporters said money not spent on deals goes back to journalism, with extra weighting for regional, small and diverse newsrooms.","The Coalition supported the package and the higher 2.75 per cent rate, but said the government took too long to act."],against:["Crossbench members said big media companies could take most of the deals, so they sought a share for small publishers and bigger grants.","The Greens said the bills leave out AI companies that use journalists' work, and moved a motion calling for action on this.","Coalition senators questioned how grants would be checked and wanted opinion writers counted as editorial staff.","Senator Alex Antic opposed the bills, saying they protect large legacy media companies rather than journalism or new players."],speeches:31,speakers:24,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-08-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29174/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-18",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29176/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_18.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29235/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7525:{overview:"This bill sets up how the news bargaining incentive will run. Large digital platforms with big search or social media services would face a charge based on their Australian digital advertising revenue, which they can reduce by making deals with Australian news businesses. The debate focused on crossbench amendments meant to help small, local and independent publishers.",for:["The government said platforms that benefit from Australian news should make a fair contribution, and should not avoid this by removing news.","The government said any money raised goes back to the news sector, and it is not about raising revenue.","The government said requiring deals with at least eight news groups, and a 200 per cent weighting for small and medium publishers, supports media diversity.","Crossbench members supported the bill's aim of fixing a gap in the earlier bargaining code."],against:["Kate Chaney moved amendments to require a share of deal spending go to publishers earning under $20 million, and to widen grant access for small outlets.","Dai Le moved an amendment so no two news groups could receive more than half of a platform's deal spending.","Allegra Spender moved amendments so deals struck with coalitions of small publishers clearly count, and raised concern there was no Senate inquiry.","Crossbench members argued that, without changes, most money could flow to the largest media companies. The government opposed the amendments and they did not pass."],speeches:12,speakers:8,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-08-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29174/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7526:{overview:"This bill makes consequential changes that support the News Media Bargaining (Administration) Bill and the News Bargaining Incentive. The Government said it sets up how the incentive will be administered, collected and enforced, and how it fits with existing tax laws. The bill passed the House without further debate.",for:["The Government said the changes are needed to run, collect and enforce the News Bargaining Incentive alongside existing tax laws.","The Government said the bill helps support a sustainable and diverse Australian news media sector."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:3,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-08-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29174/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7527:{overview:"This bill extends key counter-terrorism powers, such as control orders, preventive detention orders and special police powers, for three more years to December 2029. It also makes control orders the main tool for managing terrorist offenders after release, lets control orders on 14 to 17 year olds last up to 12 months, and changes how terrorist groups are de-listed. The debate focused on the timing of the bill, the change to post-release orders, youth radicalisation, and an amendment about recording convictions for terrorist acts.",for:["The Government said these powers must not lapse, given the 'probable' threat level and the Bondi attack in December 2025.","Government members said longer control orders for teenagers would allow longer rehabilitation programs, with courts still deciding the length and the child's best interests.","Labor speakers said better information sharing with the states and access to findings of guilt would give judges a fuller picture of risk.","The Coalition said it would not oppose the bill, as many of these powers were created by Coalition governments and must continue.","Rebekha Sharkie supported the bill, saying it would strengthen protections for the community against terrorism."],against:["Coalition speakers said Labor waited until months before the powers expired, and took over three years to respond to a key review.","The Coalition said the government must prove that replacing extended supervision orders with control orders would not weaken agencies' ability to manage offenders.","Coalition members criticised the government's handling of people linked to ISIS returning from Syria, saying the bill does nothing to address it.","Rebekha Sharkie moved an amendment so a person found guilty of a terrorist act must have a conviction recorded. The Coalition and Andrew Gee supported it.","The Government opposed that amendment, saying current sentencing law is balanced and such a discharge has never happened for a terrorist act."],speeches:14,speakers:9,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-08-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29173/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29178/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29235/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7528:{overview:"This bill sets up the News Journalism Payment Scheme, which would hand out any money raised by the News Bargaining Incentive charge to eligible Australian news organisations. Payments would be based on how many journalists an outlet employs, with extra for regional, small and diverse outlets, and set amounts for small publishers and AAP. The debate focused on whether the new scheme improves on the earlier bargaining code and how well it helps small and regional publishers.",for:["The Government said platforms profit from Australian news and should either do deals with publishers or pay a charge that goes back to journalism.","Government members said the scheme raises no revenue for government, and payments are tied to keeping journalists employed.","Labor members said a 20 per cent boost for regional, small and diverse outlets would help local news and communities often left out.","The Prime Minister said the bill closes a loophole that let platforms avoid the old code by removing news.","Coalition and independent members supported the goal, and Monique Ryan said the scheme was better than the previous code."],against:["The Coalition moved an amendment saying the scheme may not deliver more support than its earlier code, after two years of delay. It wanted a higher charge rate.","Coalition speakers called for stronger tax office powers to check platforms' advertising revenue, and raised concerns about transparency in government-run media grants.","Monique Ryan said AI companies should be included, and that the $150,000 revenue threshold and small grant stream would leave many small outlets out.","Kate Chaney moved amendments requiring a share of deals to go to smaller publishers. The Government opposed them, saying the bill already strikes the right balance.","Nationals speakers asked that regional loadings go to outlets that employ journalists, and for better data on newsroom closures."],speeches:18,speakers:16,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-08-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29174/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-18",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29176/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_18.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7529:{overview:"This bill adds $3.6 billion to the fund that pays for a 15 per cent pay rise for early childhood educators, extending the payment to June 2028 and the fund to the end of 2029. Services that take the money must limit fee rises, and from July 2027 must meet the national standard for children's health and safety or risk losing funding. All parties supported or did not oppose the bill, but the debate covered whether a temporary grant is a long-term plan, who is left out, and childcare access in regional areas.",for:["The Government said the pay rise has worked, with about 20,000 more educators, job vacancies down about 31 per cent and fewer services needing staffing waivers.","Labor speakers said tying funding to fee limits saves the average family about $1,500, and without the bill fees could rise about 17 per cent.","Government members said linking funding to the health and safety standard adds to recent child safety reforms like the worker register and mandatory training.","Labor speakers said the workforce is mostly women and has long been undervalued, so the pay rise helps close the gender pay gap.","The Government said requiring a workplace agreement makes sure the money reaches workers' pay."],against:["Coalition speakers said extending a temporary grant is not a long-term plan and creates another funding cliff in 2028.","The Coalition said most family day care educators are contractors and miss out, and moved an amendment to remove the workplace agreement requirement.","Coalition and Nationals members said the bill does not fix childcare shortages in regional areas, where many towns have no centre or long waiting lists.","The Greens said 15 per cent is not enough and called for a free universal system, while independents raised fee rules, visas for overseas educators and affordability.","Some independents said not-for-profit and community centres struggle with the fee cap and paperwork, and childcare costs keep rising faster than inflation."],speeches:135,speakers:82,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-08-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29173/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-20",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29178/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_20.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-07",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29179/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_07.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-08",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29180/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_08.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-09",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29181/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_09.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29182/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29240/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-15",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29241/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_15.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7530:{overview:"This bill makes consequential amendments linked to news journalism payments. The transcript only records the minister moving the second and third readings, with no speeches on the content of the bill.",for:[],against:[],speeches:2,speakers:1,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-08-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29174/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-19",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29177/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_19.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7534:{overview:"This bill creates a levy on licensed online wagering companies to cover the cost of new gambling rules. Those rules, in a companion bill, are expected to limit betting inducements and set up a register where people can opt out of seeing wagering ads. The bill passed the House without debate beyond the minister's speech.",for:["The minister said the wagering industry, not taxpayers, should pay the cost of regulating these activities.","The minister said a Senate inquiry heard concerns about betting inducements adding to gambling harm, which the companion reforms aim to address.","The minister said the levy would only recover the costs of running and enforcing the new rules."],against:[],speeches:3,speakers:2,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-08-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29175/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-18",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29176/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_18.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},r7540:{overview:"This bill creates new offences for importing, holding or moving illegal tobacco, which can be proven using visible signs such as non-compliant packaging or a price below the tax owed. It also tightens control of duty-free tobacco, speeds up seizures, adds licence duties for customs brokers and warehouses, and strengthens laws for taking criminals' profits. The bill had broad support, but the House debate focused mostly on whether tobacco excise should be cut.",for:["Government members said current offences need proof of intent to cheat on tax, which crime groups avoid by keeping their distance from the border.","Supporters said taking criminals' cash, houses and cars hurts syndicates more than fines, and the bill makes that easier.","Labor speakers pointed to Queensland, where stronger enforcement was followed by more legal tobacco sales and more calls to Quitline.","Coalition members said they would not oppose the bill, as it gives police and border agencies practical new tools.","Independents Monique Ryan, Zali Steggall and Sophie Scamps backed the bill, saying illegal tobacco is an enforcement problem, not a tax problem."],against:["Coalition speakers said the bill ignores the main cause: high excise creates a huge price gap that makes illegal tobacco very profitable.","The Coalition moved amendments to cut tobacco excise by 80 per cent, and One Nation's Barnaby Joyce moved for a 75 per cent cut; both were defeated.","The government, Labor members and several independents said cutting excise would make smoking cheaper and raise smoking rates, pointing to Canada's experience in 1994.","Coalition MP Mary Aldred raised concerns that the new offences reverse the usual burden of proof, and said courts rarely impose maximum penalties anyway.","Independent Allegra Spender supported the bill but asked the government to at least freeze excise and set clear goals for when it would change approach."],speeches:63,speakers:44,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-09-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29182/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29186/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-10-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29187/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_10_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-10-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29188/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_10_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1427:{overview:"This Coalition private senator's bill would stop the Housing Australia Future Fund from investing in housing projects or entities financed by Cbus Super, the construction industry super fund. Supporters linked Cbus to the CFMEU union, which is in administration. The debate focused on Cbus's ties to the CFMEU, how the fund and super funds are governed, and how many homes the fund has delivered so far.",for:["Coalition senators said Cbus is closely tied to the CFMEU, noting union board seats and payments to the union, so taxpayer funds should be kept separate.","Supporters argued CFMEU conduct has raised construction costs by up to 30 per cent, making new homes dearer.","Coalition speakers said the fund had built only 17 new homes and bought about 340 existing ones, and called for more transparency.","One Nation supported the bill until the CFMEU changes its conduct, while proposing to close the fund and set up a people's bank instead."],against:["Government senators said Cbus is not the CFMEU, and that its board has equal employer and member representatives plus independent directors.","The Government said super funds do not receive fund money; they may lend to projects, so the ban would shrink the money available for social housing.","Government speakers said the regulator APRA already oversees Cbus governance, and that Cbus has not taken part in any fund transactions so far.","Government senators described the bill as an attack on unions and industry super that would slow housing delivery.","The Greens opposed the bill, saying now is not the time to stop super funds investing in housing, and called for tax and rental reforms."],speeches:27,speakers:15,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28818/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28821/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29214/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1430:{overview:"This Greens bill would make it illegal for any company with a large degree of market power to charge an excessive price, across the whole economy. The excessive price would be measured against what a competitive market would charge. The Senate debate focused on supermarket prices, the cost of living, and whether the bill would work in practice.",for:["The Greens said Coles and Woolworths hold about two-thirds of the supermarket market, and big companies are using their market power to raise prices.","Greens senators argued price gouging also happens in banking, energy, insurance and airlines, so a ban should cover the whole economy, not just supermarkets.","Supporters said the bill follows advice from former ACCC chair Professor Allan Fels and a European model.","Greens senators argued price gouging adds to inflation and interest rate pressure, and tackling it would ease cost-of-living stress.","Senator Payman said the government's supermarket regulations were made without parliamentary scrutiny, start late, and leave out other sectors."],against:["One Nation said it supports the aim but opposes the bill, saying its wording is unclear and would mainly lead to long court cases.","One Nation senators argued existing laws should be enforced first, and that more regulation would hurt small businesses and reduce competition.","Senator Canavan argued the bill amounts to government price setting, with no clear benchmark for working out a competitive price.","Opponents warned that wrongly set prices could hurt farmers and suppliers and lead to shortages of basic goods."],speeches:11,speakers:8,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29201/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_05.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1457:{overview:"This bill makes the Australian Energy Regulator a separate government body from the ACCC, with control over its own staff, budget and governance. Its role and powers as the energy regulator stay the same. Much of the debate moved beyond the bill to power prices, renewable energy and gas supply.",for:["The Government said the regulator has grown from 15 staff in 2005 to about 400, so it needs control over its own people and money.","Labor members said the regulator's board is accountable for its work but does not control its resources, which is poor governance.","Government speakers pointed to reviews in 2015, 2017 and 2020 recommending independence, and said state and territory ministers agreed in 2023.","Labor members said an independent regulator would better protect consumers and build public trust during the energy transition.","The Coalition said it would support the bill after being assured the change would not push up power bills."],against:["Coalition speakers said the government should focus on lowering power bills, and described the bill as reshuffling the bureaucracy.","Coalition members said the government had not delivered its promised $275 cut to power bills.","Barnaby Joyce argued that renewable energy policy is raising prices and driving industry away, and questioned secret energy investment deals.","Ed Husic supported the bill but called for more frequent gas market reporting and stronger action on gas prices and contracts."],speeches:28,speakers:16,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28816/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_23_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28853/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28863/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28866/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-11-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28869/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_11_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1458:{overview:"This bill follows a review of Medicare fraud and errors. It cuts the time to lodge bulk-billed claims from two years to one, gives investigators more consistent powers across Medicare and the PBS, speeds up some pharmacy approvals, and lets the medicines regulator act before a shortage hits. All major parties supported it, but much of the debate turned to bulk-billing rates and the cost of seeing a GP.",for:["The Government said the changes respond to the Philip review and will help catch fraud and wrong claims while barely affecting honest doctors.","Labor members said a shorter claim window and clearer investigation powers will protect taxpayer money and patient safety.","Supporters said faster pharmacy approvals will help new suburbs, and the shortage power lets the regulator get ahead of supply problems.","The Coalition supported the bill, noting a Senate committee had recommended it pass.","The Greens supported the bill but asked the government to focus on educating practitioners before taking enforcement action."],against:["Monique Ryan said new powers to demand records and recover payments from doctors who were not at fault could be unfair.","Coalition speakers said the bill does nothing to fix falling bulk-billing rates and record out-of-pocket costs.","One Nation questioned the new medicines shortage power and moved to require parliamentary oversight of those approvals. It did not pass.","Pauline Hanson moved to require photo ID for Medicare claims, which the Government and Coalition did not support.","The Greens and Senator Thorpe moved amendments for a seniors dental scheme and better prison health care, which the Government opposed."],speeches:117,speakers:43,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28818/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_24_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28819/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28820/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-08-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28830/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_08_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2025-09-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/28843/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2025_09_01_Official.pdf;fileType=application%2Fpdf"}]},s1463:{overview:"This bill, from Senators Antic and Canavan, aimed to change the Sex Discrimination Act to remove references to gender identity and return to definitions based on biological sex. The debate was about whether the Senate should even allow the bill to be introduced, and Labor and the Greens voted against its introduction.",for:["Senator Antic said the bill would protect women's safety, privacy and opportunities by restoring the Act to its original focus on biological sex.","Senator Antic said the 2013 change adding gender identity had unintended effects and put people under legal threat for disagreeing with it.","Coalition Senator Scarr said bills are normally allowed past first reading so they can be properly debated, and this does not show support for the bill.","Senator Roberts said blocking the bill was an act of control driven by fear."],against:["Greens Senator McKim said the bill would give a platform to people who want to attack transgender Australians, especially trans children.","Government Senator Gallagher said Labor opposed it at first reading, as it had with a similar bill, because debate could harm individuals, especially young people.","Labor and the Greens said trans people and trans children deserve support and protection from this kind of debate."],speeches:13,speakers:6,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-07-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28822/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_07_31_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1464:{overview:"This Coalition bill would set minimum numbers of personal staff for the Opposition, larger non-government parties and independents, so the Prime Minister could give more but not less. The debate focused on recent cuts to Opposition and crossbench staff, and whether staffing should depend on the Prime Minister's choice. No government senators spoke in the debate.",for:["Coalition senators said the Prime Minister has full discretion over non-government staff, with no minimums, criteria, review or appeal.","Supporters said cutting Opposition and crossbench staff reduces their ability to read bills, consult and question the government.","One Nation, Senator Payman and Senator Babet said crossbench staff were cut unevenly, with no reasons given and no consultation.","Supporters said the bill only sets a floor and leaves the government in control of total staff numbers.","Speakers cited reviews finding staff workloads in some offices were too high and raised health and safety concerns."],against:["The Greens said they share concerns about staffing but the bill entrenches the power of the major parties.","The Greens said the model does not reflect how people voted, including the share of votes for minor parties.","The Greens said it could give independents large resources regardless of workload and encourage members to leave their parties.","The Greens moved an amendment calling on the government to work with everyone on a fair, independent staffing model."],speeches:23,speakers:13,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-08-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28831/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_08_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-08-28",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28841/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_08_28_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28850/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_03_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1468:{overview:"This Coalition bill would let either house of parliament disallow, or reject, the minister's directions for Housing Australia, which cover programs like the Home Guarantee Scheme. The debate focused on the government's expansion of the 5 per cent deposit scheme and whether parliament should be able to review such changes.",for:["Coalition senators said the minister can make big changes to housing programs through an instrument that parliament cannot disallow.","Coalition and One Nation speakers said removing income and place caps on the deposit scheme is pushing up entry-level house prices.","Supporters cited the Senate scrutiny committee and the Law Reform Commission, saying major policy should face parliamentary oversight.","The Greens said they support stronger accountability and want an end to housing policy made only through non-disallowable instruments.","Supporters said parliament's role is to check the government, not simply approve its decisions."],against:["Government senators said the bill would let the Senate block the 5 per cent deposit scheme, Help to Buy and the Housing Australia Future Fund.","Labor speakers said first home buyers could again face larger deposits and about $23,000 in lenders mortgage insurance.","The government said investment mandates need stability, and political uncertainty could deter builders and investors and delay social housing.","Government senators said voters backed Labor's housing plan, and cited Treasury saying the scheme's effect on prices is modest.","The Greens said the bill alone would not fix housing problems and called for more direct public housing."],speeches:84,speakers:20,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28853/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28867/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_30_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-02-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29200/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_02_04.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29214/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_26.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1470:{overview:"This private senator's bill, introduced by Coalition Senator Jane Hume, would let couples voluntarily move money from the partner with the larger super balance to the other partner, to even up their balances. It includes limits so balances are only evened up and caps are not exceeded. The debate focused on whether it would help close the gap between women's and men's super, or mainly help wealthier couples.",for:["Coalition senators said women retire with 20 to 25 per cent less super, often after time out of work caring for children or family.","Supporters said couples can already split super when they divorce, so they should be able to do it while together.","Coalition speakers said the plan is voluntary, uses existing rollover rules, costs taxpayers nothing and has safeguards against tax gaming.","Supporters said it could help older women, who are the fastest growing group facing homelessness, and works for any couple whichever partner earns more.","One Nation said it supported the bill because it supports families and lets couples manage their savings together."],against:["Labor senators said the bill would mostly benefit a small number of wealthy couples and could be used to lower tax.","Government senators said it would not help single women, including older single women most at risk of poverty in retirement.","Labor said it leaves women relying on their partner, and that measures like super on paid parental leave tackle the causes of the gap.","The Greens said it would not fix structural problems such as low-income earners getting smaller tax benefits on super, and could complicate splits at separation.","A Labor senator said it could allow gaming of preservation rules and social security eligibility."],speeches:39,speakers:19,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-09-04",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28853/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_09_04_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-05",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28876/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_05_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-05-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29219/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_05_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-24",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29223/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_24.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1472:{overview:"This private senator's bill, introduced by independent Senator David Pocock, would create a legal duty for decision-makers to consider the health and wellbeing of current and future Australian children when approving projects that add greenhouse gas emissions. For coal, oil and gas projects, it would stop approvals likely to pose a material risk of harm to children. The debate focused on a court case brought by young people, climate risks, and whether the Government's current climate policies are enough.",for:["Senator Pocock said a court found the duty of care was a matter for parliament to legislate, and this bill does that.","Supporters said a Senate inquiry received 403 submissions with only one outright opposed, and doctors and young people backed the bill.","Greens senators said health checks are already standard for major projects, so considering children's health in climate decisions is common sense.","Supporters, including Senator Payman, said decisions such as new coal and gas approvals show the need for long-term thinking in law.","Liberal Senator Andrew McLachlan, speaking for himself, supported the bill and said caring for the environment is a conservative value."],against:["Government Senator Michelle Ananda-Rajah did not back the bill and said the Government's focus is on how to reach net zero, not whether to.","She said Australians want an orderly shift away from fossil fuels, without blackouts or high bills, so jobs and industry are protected.","She said existing Government policies, such as emissions targets, the safeguard mechanism and renewable energy schemes, are already driving change.","Senator Pocock said the Government gave only a short response to the inquiry report, and a Coalition senator interjected that workers were losing jobs."],speeches:25,speakers:12,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-10-29",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28863/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_10_29_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1478:{overview:"This bill gives the NDIS Quality and Safeguards Commission stronger powers, including much higher penalties, wider banning orders that cover auditors and consultants, and new orders to stop misleading promotion of NDIS services. It also adds a 90-day cooling-off period for people leaving the scheme, electronic claim forms, and clarifies that plan changes can raise or lower funding. The Senate added whistleblower protections and fixed a gap about who can withdraw a participant, and the debate focused on fraud, safety and participant rights.",for:["Government members said the royal commission and NDIS review showed abuse, neglect and fraud, and the commission needs stronger powers to act early.","Labor speakers said tougher penalties and jail terms will deter providers who harm participants or ignore banning orders.","The Coalition, Greens and One Nation supported the bill's direction, including anti-promotion orders and the cooling-off period.","The minister said many penalties and banning orders apply to unregistered providers too, and the government reviews far more claims than before.","The Greens said new whistleblower protections, supported by the government, will help workers and participants speak up safely."],against:["The Coalition said the bill does not go far enough and gives no direct fraud controls for the 94 per cent of providers who are unregistered.","Coalition speakers said the government cannot measure the scale of fraud and must do more, and moved a second reading amendment saying so.","Independent Monique Ryan raised concerns that the agency could cut plan funding without a new assessment, and called for review rights and advocacy.","The Greens moved amendments to protect privacy in information requests and to restore the 'reasonable and necessary' test for supports.","Senator Lidia Thorpe moved an amendment to undo 2024 rules stopping people with some past convictions managing NDIS plans, saying they hurt First Nations families."],speeches:40,speakers:25,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28888/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-03-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29152/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_31.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-31",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29216/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_31.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1479:{overview:"This Greens bill would lower the maximum student fees for law, accounting, economics, commerce, communications, society and culture, and arts units back to levels set before the Job-Ready Graduates changes of 2020. Supporters said it would roughly halve the cost of an arts degree. The debate focused on student debt, fairness for low-income students, and the cost to university funding.",for:["The Greens said fees for arts, humanities, law and social science degrees had more than doubled, leaving students with debts of over $50,000.","Supporters said enrolments by low-income students fell, especially in the courses with the highest fees.","The Greens said almost all submissions to the Senate inquiry supported reversing the fee rises urgently.","Independent Senator David Pocock said the scheme had not worked and needed urgent reform, though he did not agree with every part of the bill.","Supporters argued the government could afford to fund universities properly by choosing different spending and tax priorities."],against:["Government senators said evidence to the Senate committee showed the bill would cut university funding and could hurt courses, staff, research and regional providers.","The government said it was already reducing student debt and changing funding, and would keep reforming through the new tertiary education commission.","Coalition speakers said the bill would remove about $1.3 billion a year from universities with no way to replace the money.","The Coalition argued the bill ignored that the scheme lowered fees for nursing, teaching, agriculture and science, creating a new and uneven fee structure.","Coalition speakers said the scheme should be reviewed as a whole, with any reform properly funded and designed."],speeches:8,speakers:6,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28885/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_25_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29227/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1482:{overview:"This private senator's bill would bring back a conscientious objection option, removed in 2015 under the No Jab No Pay policy. Parents who formally declare an objection to childhood vaccination, after talking with an immunisation provider, would stay eligible for Family Tax Benefit Part A and the Child Care Subsidy. The debate focused on parents' choice and consent, the financial effect on lower-income families, and the effect on vaccination rates and disease.",for:["Senator Antic, a Liberal, said decisions about vaccination should rest with parents, and that tying payments to vaccination is coercive.","Supporters argued the policy mostly affects lower-income families who rely on payments, while wealthier families can object without losing money.","Senator Babet said the bill does not ban vaccines or stop doctors recommending them, and that most children who caught up were not from objecting families.","One Nation and Senator Whitten said consent must be free from pressure, and linked their concerns to COVID-era mandates."],against:["Government senators said childhood vaccination saves lives and the bill would lower vaccination rates, raising the risk of diseases like measles and whooping cough.","The Government said no-one is forced to vaccinate, medical exemptions already exist, and it would not widen exemptions.","Coalition senators said the policy helped lift five-year-old coverage above 95 per cent and protects babies, immunocompromised children and pregnant women.","The Coalition noted vaccination coverage has been falling and whooping cough cases are high, so reopening objections now would speed that decline.","The Greens opposed the bill but said the policy can penalise low-income families, and called for better access to GPs and an independent review."],speeches:11,speakers:9,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2025-11-26",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/28888/toc_pdf/Senate_2025_11_26_Official.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-16",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29242/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_16.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1488:{overview:"This Coalition bill would make it a crime, with up to 10 years in jail, to help someone with terrorism links enter Australia unless both the Home Affairs and Foreign Affairs ministers give written approval. It would also extend the 'declared areas' offence to 2030. The debate centred on the possible return from Syria of a group of Australian women and children linked to Islamic State.",for:["The Coalition said the Government was leaving these returns to charities and private individuals, and that ministers should approve and take responsibility for them.","Coalition senators said the people involved chose to travel to a terrorist area and could pose a security risk, adding to pressure on ASIO.","Supporters said the bill would not stop citizens coming home or apply to past returns, and ministers could still approve humanitarian cases.","Coalition senators raised questions about how passports were issued and carried to the camps, and spoke of Yazidi and Assyrian communities' fears.","One Nation supported the bill, saying it would force any returns into the open and raised concerns about cost and safety."],against:["The Government said the bill was badly drafted, did not cover the group it claimed to target, and could make pilots, baggage handlers, aid workers and allies criminals.","The Government said it was not helping anyone return and that security agencies were ready to act if people came back on their own.","Government members said fighters had returned under the former Coalition government without complaint from the Coalition.","The Greens opposed the bill, saying it would punish children stuck in camps and the charities trying to help them, and that it was unconstitutional.","Greens senators said some women were trafficked or forced to go as young girls, and that Australia should bring its citizens home."],speeches:40,speakers:19,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29141/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-03",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29205/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_03.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-03-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29209/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_03_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-04-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29217/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_04_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1493:{overview:"This bill lets the Treasurer declare 'exceptional circumstances', such as the fuel crisis linked to the conflict involving Iran, so the ACCC can quickly allow businesses to work together in ways competition law would normally ban. It also sets much higher penalties for breaking the Oil Code of Conduct. The debate focused on whether the new powers were needed, how much parliament could oversee them, and why they could apply back to 1 April 2026.",for:["The Government said the current approval process is too slow, noting COVID-era supermarket approvals took months, and the new powers let businesses act early to prevent shortages.","Government members said the Treasurer's declaration can be disallowed by parliament and lasts six months, with time-limited extensions.","Supporters said penalties of up to $10 million or more would stop fuel companies treating breaches as a cost of doing business.","The Greens supported the bill and won an amendment making ACCC class exemptions disallowable by parliament.","The Government said the ACCC supported the bill and that the backdating covers coordination meetings held since the conflict began."],against:["The Coalition said existing ACCC powers worked during COVID, with some approvals given in under 24 hours, so new powers were not shown to be needed.","Coalition and One Nation senators said the Treasurer's power is not limited to the fuel crisis and is permanent, with no sunset date.","Opponents questioned the backdating to 1 April, saying it could excuse possible anticompetitive conduct by fuel companies early in the crisis.","Opposition speakers objected that the bill was rushed without a committee inquiry and that exemptions need only be published within seven days.","Independent Helen Haines said she was not convinced the bill was proportionate and could not support it without more scrutiny or changes."],speeches:53,speakers:27,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2026-05-13",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29219/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_05_13.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-05-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29220/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_05_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-05-25",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29157/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_05_25.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1496:{overview:"This private senators' bill would change the Aged Care Act so that a trained assessor can override the Integrated Assessment Tool, a computer tool used to set older people's care levels. It would also require decision notices to explain how the tool was used and let people assessed since November 2025 ask for a fresh assessment. The debate focused on whether assessors should have this discretion and what it would mean for fairness, costs and wait times; it passed the Senate, and in the House the Coalition tried unsuccessfully to bring it on for immediate debate.",for:["Coalition senators said a qualified assessor's clinical judgement should not be overruled by a computer tool.","Supporters said review requests rose sharply after the tool started, and geriatric doctors had called it a clinical safety risk.","The Greens argued the tool was not clinically tested or trialled on people, and said it was designed to reduce care costs.","Independent Senator Pocock said assessors can only rubberstamp the tool's result, and cited robodebt royal commission advice on human oversight.","Coalition members in the House shared local cases where they said older people did not get the care they needed."],against:["Government senators said they shared the bill's intent but could not support it as drafted, saying it could be counterproductive.","The Government argued broad assessor discretion under the old system led to very different outcomes for people with similar needs.","Government speakers cited departmental advice that the bill would have significant costs and could lengthen wait times by about five months.","The Government said it would instead legislate an escalation pathway so tool outcomes could be changed in extenuating circumstances."],speeches:18,speakers:12,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-23",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29222/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_23.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-02",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29228/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_02.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-08-11",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29172/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_08_11.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1499:{overview:"This Greens bill, introduced by Senator Nick McKim, would create a new commissioner at the Australian Human Rights Commission for sexual orientation, gender identity and sex characteristics. It would also replace the term 'intersex status' in the law with 'innate variations of sex characteristics'. The transcript covers only its introduction and the start of the second reading speech.",for:["Senator McKim said these are the only protected attributes in the main federal anti-discrimination laws without their own commissioner.","He said the bill would treat LGBTIQA+ people the same as other groups with protected attributes.","He said the wording change follows a 2017 statement by intersex organisations and matches changes in several states and territories."],against:[],speeches:10,speakers:2,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2026-06-30",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29226/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_06_30.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1500:{overview:"This bill, introduced by Coalition Senator Michaelia Cash, would change the Sex Discrimination Act 1984. The transcript only covers the procedure for introducing the bill in the Senate, and no arguments about its content were made.",for:[],against:[],speeches:5,speakers:3,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2026-07-01",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29227/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_07_01.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1503:{overview:"This One Nation bill would let the Federal Court make orders keeping foreign terrorist fighters out of Australia, tighten bail for terrorism charges, allow passports to be refused or cancelled, and make it an offence to help bring such people back. The debate focused on the return of women linked to Islamic State, whether current exclusion laws are enough, and whether the bill is constitutional.",for:["One Nation said current temporary exclusion orders only delay and manage a return, while this bill would let a court keep the most dangerous people out.","Supporters said safeguards include court decisions, two-year limits, review rights, a voluntary-conduct test and extra protections for people under 18.","One Nation argued the government had not shown legal advice that the bill was unconstitutional, and said no inquiry submitter found it invalid.","Coalition speakers said they would support the bill because doing something was better than doing nothing, while noting some concerns could be worked through.","Supporters said stricter bail rules were needed because terrorism is not an ordinary crime."],against:["The Greens said the bill would likely be struck down by the courts and was about politics rather than workable policy.","The Greens argued that funding international trials to prosecute Islamic State fighters would do more to keep people safe.","Independent senator David Pocock said terms like 'protect the Australian community' were too vague and it was unclear what the new orders added.","Senator Pocock said the inquiry was too short, with two weeks for submissions and no public hearings.","Senator Pocock raised concerns about constitutionality and about dividing the community."],speeches:9,speakers:7,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2026-08-12",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29230/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_08_12.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-10",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29239/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_10.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1517:{overview:"This private members' bill, introduced by independent Sophie Scamps in the House and by Senator David Pocock with Senators Faruqi, Payman and Thorpe in the Senate, aims to stop Australia arming, funding or investing in genocide, war crimes or illegal settlements. It would tighten defence export checks, restrict investments by the Future Fund, super funds and charities, create an Anti-Genocide Commissioner and genocide risk reporting, and make it an offence to trade in settlement goods and services. Only supporters spoke before debate was adjourned.",for:["Supporters said there are gaps in Australian law, such as no requirement for defence export decisions to assess the risk of contributing to genocide or war crimes.","They said public money and retirement savings should not flow to companies linked to illegal settlements or serious breaches of international law, and should be divested.","Senator Pocock said the rules are not about picking a side in one conflict but about applying international law consistently.","Supporters said an independent Anti-Genocide Commissioner would give scrutiny based on evidence and law rather than the politics of the day.","They said other countries such as Spain, Ireland and Slovenia have already moved to put similar obligations into their own laws."],against:[],speeches:7,speakers:5,days:[{house:"House",date:"2026-09-14",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29183/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_14.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-15",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29241/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_15.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]},s1518:{overview:"This private senator's bill, from Senator Lambie, would stop the government putting a dollar or number limit on treatment for veterans' accepted service-related injuries, so care depends on clinical need. The debate centred on a proposed $5,000 allied health threshold, which the government announced it was dropping on the day of the debate.",for:["Senator Lambie said veterans who need treatment because of their service should get it, without hitting a set spending or visit limit.","Supporters pointed to the Royal Commission into Defence and Veteran Suicide, which said timely health care is key to reducing veteran suicide.","Coalition, Greens and crossbench senators said a law was needed so a cap could not simply be brought back later.","Speakers said long claim processing times at Veterans' Affairs meant asking for approval beyond a threshold would delay care.","Senator Lambie said the bill still allows clinical oversight and safeguards against fraud and poor provider conduct."],against:["Government senators said they would not support the bill because the $5,000 threshold had now been withdrawn.","The government said the way the bill is drafted could have unintended consequences.","The government said the threshold was meant to cut paperwork, replacing GP re-referrals every 12 visits, alongside the biggest allied health fee rise in 20 years."],speeches:25,speakers:13,days:[{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-15",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29241/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_15.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"House",date:"2026-09-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansardr/29186/toc_pdf/House%20of%20Representatives_2026_09_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"},{house:"Senate",date:"2026-09-17",url:"https://parlinfo.aph.gov.au/parlInfo/download/chamber/hansards/29243/toc_pdf/Senate_2026_09_17.pdf;fileType=application/pdf"}]}}}},1066,[]);
